/* Community Medicine 5–6: NCDs & nutrition; environment & occupational health */
Z("cm5",0,"cm",["Hypertension (BP ≥ 140/90 mmHg) is a common, often silent, non-communicable disease affecting about one in four Indian adults; it is a major risk factor for stroke, heart disease and kidney failure, and is prevented and controlled by population-wide lifestyle measures, screening and sustained treatment.","Hypertension is the 'silent killer' — most people don't know they have it until a stroke or heart attack; regular BP checks and salt reduction are the key.",["Burden: NFHS-5 — ~24% men, 21% women (≥ 15 yr); ICMR-INDIAB 35% adults; 'rule of halves' — half aware, half of those treated, half of those controlled; leading risk for CVD deaths.","Risk factors: non-modifiable — age, sex, genetics, ethnicity; modifiable — high salt (> 5 g/day), obesity, physical inactivity, alcohol, tobacco, low potassium/fruit-vegetable intake, stress, diabetes, dyslipidaemia.","Prevention: population strategy (salt reduction, healthy diet — DASH, physical activity, weight control, tobacco/alcohol control, food labelling), high-risk strategy (screening ≥ 30 years — NP-NCD, opportunistic BP checks), primordial prevention in children.","Control: India Hypertension Control Initiative (IHCI, 2017) — standard treatment protocols, drug supply, team-based care, patient-centred follow-up, information systems; '75 by 25' (75 million on standard care by 2025); target 25% relative reduction in prevalence by 2025."],"Rule of halves in hypertension: ½ detected, ½ treated, ½ controlled."],
"Hypertension is defined as systolic BP ≥ 140 mmHg and/or diastolic ≥ 90 mmHg (JNC/WHO/ISH; ACC/AHA 2017 uses ≥ 130/80) or current antihypertensive treatment. Burden: affects ~1.3 billion adults worldwide (WHO 2023), two-thirds in LMICs; leading cause of cardiovascular deaths (~10 million/year); in India, prevalence ~25–30% in adults (NFHS-5: 24% men and 21% women aged ≥ 15 years with elevated BP or on medication; ICMR-INDIAB 2023: 35%); rising in rural areas and younger age groups; only ~37% aware, ~30% treated and ~15% controlled. Epidemiology: 'rule of halves' (Wilber) — half of hypertensives in the community are undetected, half of the detected are untreated, and half of the treated are inadequately controlled; 'tracking' of BP from childhood to adulthood; iceberg phenomenon. Classification by aetiology: primary (essential, 90–95%) and secondary (renal, endocrine, coarctation, drugs, pregnancy). Risk factors: (1) non-modifiable — age (BP rises with age), sex (males before 50, females after menopause), genetic factors and family history, ethnicity (African origin, South Asians); (2) modifiable — high salt (sodium) intake (Indians consume ~10–11 g/day vs WHO recommended < 5 g), low potassium intake (fruits, vegetables), obesity and central adiposity (each 10 kg weight gain raises SBP by ~3 mmHg), physical inactivity, alcohol consumption, tobacco use, high saturated fat diet, psychosocial stress, socioeconomic factors, urbanisation, environmental noise, air pollution, diabetes, dyslipidaemia, sleep apnoea. Complications: stroke (most important in Asians), coronary heart disease, heart failure, left ventricular hypertrophy, chronic kidney disease, retinopathy, peripheral vascular disease, aortic dissection, dementia. Prevention: (1) primordial prevention — prevent emergence of risk factors in children and young adults (healthy diet, physical activity, avoiding tobacco, school health programmes); (2) primary prevention — (a) population (mass) strategy — shift the population distribution of BP downwards: salt reduction (food reformulation, front-of-pack labelling, Eat Right India campaign, consumer education), increasing fruit and vegetable intake, weight reduction and control of obesity, regular physical activity (≥ 150 min/week moderate), moderation of alcohol, tobacco control (COTPA, NTCP), stress management, trans-fat elimination; DASH diet; (b) high-risk strategy — identify individuals at high risk (family history, obesity, prehypertension, diabetes) and modify risk factors; (3) secondary prevention — early detection by screening (population-based screening of all adults ≥ 30 years every year under NP-NCD at Ayushman Arogya Mandirs; opportunistic screening in all OPD visits), confirmation and treatment with lifestyle modification and antihypertensive drugs, regular follow-up, adherence, control of other risk factors (absolute CV risk approach); (4) tertiary prevention — management of complications, rehabilitation after stroke and MI. Programmes: National Programme for Prevention and Control of Non-Communicable Diseases (NP-NCD; formerly NPCDCS, 2010) — NCD clinics at district and CHC levels, screening at sub-centres/HWCs by ANMs and CHOs, NCD portal; India Hypertension Control Initiative (IHCI, 2017; ICMR, MoHFW, WHO) — five components: standard (simple) treatment protocols (amlodipine, telmisartan, chlorthalidone), assured supply of drugs, team-based care and task sharing, patient-centred services (multi-month dispensing, follow-up near home), information systems (Simple app); '75/25 initiative' (2023) — put 75 million people with hypertension or diabetes on standard care by 2025; National Multisectoral Action Plan for NCDs — targets of 25% reduction in premature NCD mortality and 25% relative reduction in raised BP prevalence by 2025; World Hypertension Day (17 May).",
[["Risk factors",[["Non-modifiable","Modifiable"],["Age","High salt, low potassium"],["Sex","Obesity, physical inactivity"],["Genetics/family history","Alcohol, tobacco"],["Ethnicity","Stress, diabetes, dyslipidaemia"]]],
["Prevention strategies",[["Level","Measures"],["Primordial","Healthy lifestyle in children, school programmes"],["Primary — population","Salt < 5 g/day, DASH diet, physical activity, weight control, tobacco/alcohol control"],["Primary — high-risk","Target obese, prehypertensive, family history"],["Secondary","Screening ≥ 30 yr (NP-NCD), treatment, follow-up (IHCI)"],["Tertiary","Manage stroke, MI, CKD; rehabilitation"]]]],
{c:"Hypertension is a highly prevalent, under-controlled risk factor for CVD in India; population salt reduction and lifestyle change combined with screening and protocol-based treatment under NP-NCD and IHCI are the cornerstones of control."});

Z("cm5",1,"cm",["Diabetes mellitus is a chronic metabolic disease with high blood glucose; India has over 10 crore people with diabetes (ICMR-INDIAB 11.4%), mostly type 2, driven by obesity, physical inactivity, unhealthy diets and genetic susceptibility; prevention focuses on lifestyle change and early screening.","India is called the 'diabetes capital of the world' — South Asians develop diabetes at a younger age and lower BMI because of central (belly) fat and insulin resistance.",["Burden: global 53.7 crore adults (IDF 2021); India ~10.1 crore diabetes + 13.6 crore prediabetes (ICMR-INDIAB 2023); prevalence higher urban, southern states (Goa, Kerala, Puducherry); about half undiagnosed.","Types: type 1 (autoimmune, children), type 2 (> 90%), gestational, other specific (MODY, pancreatic — fibrocalculous).","Risk factors (type 2): age > 30, family history, obesity (especially central — waist > 90 cm men, > 80 cm women), sedentary lifestyle, diet high in refined carbs/fat, low fibre, urbanisation, previous GDM, hypertension, dyslipidaemia, 'thin-fat Indian' phenotype, low birth weight (Barker hypothesis), stress, tobacco.","Prevention & control: primordial/primary — healthy diet, exercise (150 min/week), weight loss (5–7% — DPP, IDPP showed 30–58% risk reduction); secondary — screening ≥ 30 yr (NP-NCD; random blood glucose), IDRS (Indian Diabetes Risk Score), HbA1c/FPG diagnosis; tertiary — prevent complications (retinopathy, nephropathy, neuropathy, foot), self-care education; World Diabetes Day 14 November."],"IDRS: age, waist, family history, physical activity (score ≥ 60 = high risk)."],
"Diabetes mellitus is a group of metabolic disorders characterised by chronic hyperglycaemia resulting from defects in insulin secretion, insulin action or both. Diagnostic criteria (WHO/ADA): fasting plasma glucose ≥ 126 mg/dL, 2-h plasma glucose ≥ 200 mg/dL during 75 g OGTT, HbA1c ≥ 6.5%, or random glucose ≥ 200 mg/dL with symptoms; prediabetes — impaired fasting glucose (100/110–125), impaired glucose tolerance (2-h 140–199), HbA1c 5.7–6.4%. Burden: IDF Diabetes Atlas 2021 — 537 million adults (20–79 years) with diabetes worldwide, projected 783 million by 2045; India — second largest number after China; ICMR-INDIAB study 2023 — prevalence 11.4% (~10.1 crore people) with prediabetes 15.3% (~13.6 crore); urban 16.4% vs rural 8.9%; highest in Goa (26%), Puducherry, Kerala; rising in rural areas and less developed states; ~50% unaware of their condition; diabetes is a leading cause of blindness, kidney failure, lower-limb amputation, heart attacks and strokes; economic burden large (out-of-pocket). Types: type 1 (autoimmune β-cell destruction; ~5%; childhood/adolescence; insulin-dependent), type 2 (~90–95%; insulin resistance with relative insulin deficiency; adults, increasingly young), gestational diabetes (onset/first recognition in pregnancy; India ~10–14%), other specific types — monogenic (MODY), diseases of exocrine pancreas (fibrocalculous pancreatic diabetes — tropical), endocrinopathies, drug-induced (steroids), malnutrition-modulated. Epidemiological determinants and risk factors for type 2: (1) host — age (> 30–45; Indians develop diabetes ~10 years earlier than Western populations), genetic predisposition (family history — risk 2–6 fold; concordance in identical twins ~90%), ethnicity (Asian Indian phenotype — higher body fat, central/visceral adiposity, insulin resistance and lower β-cell reserve at lower BMI — 'thin-fat Indian'), obesity (BMI ≥ 23 in Asians) and central obesity (waist circumference ≥ 90 cm in men, ≥ 80 cm in women), sedentary lifestyle and physical inactivity, diet (high calorie, refined carbohydrates — white rice, sugar, sugar-sweetened beverages; saturated and trans fats; low fibre), history of gestational diabetes or macrosomic baby, polycystic ovary syndrome, hypertension, dyslipidaemia, prediabetes, low birth weight and fetal undernutrition followed by rapid childhood weight gain (Barker's thrifty phenotype hypothesis; DOHaD), tobacco, alcohol, psychosocial stress, sleep deprivation, socioeconomic transition; (2) environment — urbanisation, mechanisation, changes in dietary patterns (nutrition transition), reduced physical activity, air pollution. Prevention and control: (1) primordial prevention — preventing emergence of obesity and unhealthy lifestyles in children (school health, healthy food environment, Fit India Movement, restricting junk food marketing); (2) primary prevention — population strategy: healthy diet, physical activity, weight control (Eat Right India, FSSAI regulations); high-risk strategy: identify high-risk persons (prediabetes, obesity, family history) and intensive lifestyle modification — Diabetes Prevention Program (USA — 58% reduction with lifestyle, 31% with metformin), Finnish DPS, Indian Diabetes Prevention Programme (IDPP — 28.5% reduction with lifestyle, 26.4% with metformin), Da Qing study; targets — 5–7% weight loss, ≥ 150 min/week moderate activity, diet low in refined carbohydrates and fats, high fibre; (3) secondary prevention — early detection and treatment: screening — population-based screening of all individuals ≥ 30 years under NP-NCD (random capillary blood glucose by ANM/CHO at Ayushman Arogya Mandir; confirmation at PHC), Indian Diabetes Risk Score (IDRS — Madras Diabetes Research Foundation; age, waist circumference, physical activity, family history; score ≥ 60 high risk) for targeted screening, opportunistic screening, screening of pregnant women for GDM (DIPSI — 75 g glucose non-fasting, 2-h ≥ 140 mg/dL); treatment with diet, exercise, metformin and other drugs, insulin; control of BP and lipids; regular monitoring (HbA1c); (4) tertiary prevention — prevent and manage complications: retinopathy screening (annual fundus exam), nephropathy screening (urine albumin, eGFR), foot care education and examination (monofilament), neuropathy, cardiovascular risk reduction (statins, aspirin in secondary prevention), diabetes self-management education, rehabilitation of amputees and visually impaired. Programmes: NP-NCD (screening, NCD clinics, free drugs including insulin through PHCs and Jan Aushadhi), National Programme for Health Care of the Elderly, Ayushman Bharat; National Multisectoral Action Plan — target halt the rise in diabetes and obesity by 2025; '75/25' initiative; World Diabetes Day (14 November — Banting's birthday).",
[["Risk factors (type 2)",[["Category","Factors"],["Non-modifiable","Age, family history, ethnicity (Asian Indian), previous GDM, LBW"],["Modifiable","Obesity (central), physical inactivity, unhealthy diet, tobacco, alcohol, stress, sleep"],["Associated","Hypertension, dyslipidaemia, PCOS, prediabetes"]]],
["Prevention",[["Level","Measures"],["Primordial","Healthy environment for children, Fit India, Eat Right India"],["Primary","Lifestyle modification (IDPP — 28.5% ↓), weight loss 5–7%, exercise 150 min/week"],["Secondary","Screening ≥ 30 yr (NP-NCD), IDRS, early treatment"],["Tertiary","Retinopathy, nephropathy, foot screening; complication management"]]],
["IDRS (Indian Diabetes Risk Score)",[["Parameter","Scoring"],["Age","< 35: 0; 35–49: 20; ≥ 50: 30"],["Waist (M/F)","< 80/90: 0; 80–89/90–99: 10; ≥ 90/100: 20"],["Physical activity","Vigorous: 0 … sedentary: 30"],["Family history","None: 0; one parent: 10; both: 20"],["Interpretation","≥ 60 high risk; 30–50 moderate; < 30 low"]]]],
{c:"Diabetes has reached epidemic proportions in India, driven by central obesity, inactivity and dietary transition; lifestyle-based prevention, risk-score–guided and population screening, and complication prevention under NP-NCD form the public health response."});

Z("cm5",2,"cm",["Cancer is a leading cause of death in India (~14–15 lakh new cases/year); common cancers are oral, lung, breast, cervix and stomach; about one-third are preventable (tobacco control, HPV and hepatitis B vaccination) and another third are curable if detected early — the basis of population-based screening for oral, breast and cervical cancers under NP-NCD.","Cancer control is 'prevent, detect early, treat, palliate': stop tobacco, vaccinate, screen for the three common cancers, treat in time, and relieve suffering.",["Burden: ~15.6 lakh new cases (2024 est.), ~8 lakh deaths; men — oral/lip, lung, oesophagus, stomach; women — breast (most common), cervix, ovary; tobacco causes ~40% of cancers in men; North-East highest incidence.","Prevention: tobacco control (COTPA, NTCP, quitline 1800-11-2356), alcohol control, healthy diet, physical activity, HPV vaccine (girls 9–14), hepatitis B vaccine, H. pylori, occupational and environmental carcinogen control.","Screening (NP-NCD, ≥ 30 years at HWCs): oral cancer — oral visual examination (OVE); breast — clinical breast examination (CBE) for women ≥ 30 (5-yearly); cervix — visual inspection with acetic acid (VIA) every 5 years (30–65 yr), HPV DNA test; suspicious → referral.","NPCDCS (2010) → NP-NCD (2023): cancer care — district day-care chemotherapy, State Cancer Institutes, Tertiary Cancer Care Centres, National Cancer Grid, PMJAY coverage; population-based cancer registries (ICMR-NCDIR); palliative care (NPPC)."],"NP-NCD cancer screening: Oral (OVE), Breast (CBE), Cervix (VIA) — ≥ 30 years."],
"Cancer is a group of diseases characterised by uncontrolled growth and spread of abnormal cells. Burden: globally ~2 crore new cases and 97 lakh deaths (GLOBOCAN 2022); India — ~14.6 lakh new cases in 2022 projected to ~15.7 lakh by 2025 (ICMR-NCDIR National Cancer Registry Programme), ~9 lakh deaths; India ranks third in number of cases; cumulative risk ~1 in 9 Indians in lifetime; leading sites — males: lip/oral cavity, lung, oesophagus, stomach, colorectum; females: breast (most common, ~28%), cervix uteri (second; declining), ovary, oral cavity, corpus uteri; childhood cancers (leukaemia); geographical variation — highest incidence in North-Eastern states (Aizawl — stomach, oesophagus, lung, nasopharynx), gallbladder cancer in Gangetic belt, oral cancer in central India; ~70% present in advanced stages. Risk factors: tobacco (smoked and smokeless — gutkha, khaini, betel quid with tobacco; responsible for ~27% of all cancers in India and ~40% in men — oral, lung, oesophagus, larynx, pharynx, bladder, pancreas), alcohol, infections (HPV — cervical, oropharyngeal; hepatitis B and C — liver; H. pylori — stomach; EBV — nasopharyngeal, lymphoma; HIV), diet (low fruit and vegetables, high red/processed meat, salted/smoked foods, aflatoxins), obesity and physical inactivity (breast, colon, endometrium), reproductive factors (early menarche, late first pregnancy, nulliparity, lack of breastfeeding — breast cancer; early marriage, multiparity, poor genital hygiene — cervical cancer), occupational exposures (asbestos, benzene, aromatic amines, silica), environmental (air pollution, radiation, arsenic in water), genetic predisposition (BRCA1/2), ageing. Cancer control strategies (WHO): (1) primary prevention — tobacco control: Cigarettes and Other Tobacco Products Act (COTPA 2003 — ban on smoking in public places, advertising, sale to minors and near schools, pictorial health warnings 85%), National Tobacco Control Programme (2007), tobacco cessation centres, mCessation, quitline (1800-112-356), Prohibition of Electronic Cigarettes Act 2019, taxation; WHO FCTC; alcohol control; promotion of healthy diet and physical activity, weight control; vaccination — hepatitis B (UIP birth dose) and HPV vaccine (quadrivalent/indigenous Cervavac for girls 9–14 years — national rollout); H. pylori eradication; safe sex; occupational safety; reduction of air pollution; (2) early detection — (a) early diagnosis (awareness of warning signs — CAUTION: change in bowel/bladder habits, a sore that does not heal, unusual bleeding/discharge, thickening or lump, indigestion/difficulty swallowing, obvious change in wart/mole, nagging cough/hoarseness) and (b) screening of asymptomatic population — under NP-NCD (Population-Based Screening, 2016 operational guidelines), all individuals aged ≥ 30 years are screened at Ayushman Arogya Mandirs (sub-health centres/PHCs) by ANMs/CHOs for: oral cancer — oral visual examination (OVE) every 5 years; breast cancer — clinical breast examination (CBE) every 5 years for women 30–65 years (mammography not recommended for mass screening in India due to cost and younger age profile; used for diagnosis and high-risk); cervical cancer — visual inspection with acetic acid (VIA) every 5 years for women 30–65 years (screen-and-treat with cryotherapy/thermal ablation for VIA-positive eligible lesions), Pap smear and HPV DNA testing where available (WHO 90-70-90 targets for elimination of cervical cancer by 2030: 90% girls vaccinated by 15 years, 70% women screened with high-performance test by 35 and 45, 90% of women with precancer/cancer treated); screen-positive cases referred to PHC/CHC/district hospital for confirmation (biopsy, colposcopy, FNAC); (3) treatment — surgery, radiotherapy, chemotherapy; strengthening of tertiary cancer care — State Cancer Institutes and Tertiary Cancer Care Centres (Strengthening of Tertiary Care for Cancer scheme), day-care chemotherapy centres in district hospitals (2025 budget), National Cancer Institute (Jhajjar), Tata Memorial Centre and National Cancer Grid (> 300 centres — standard guidelines), Ayushman Bharat PMJAY covering cancer packages, Health Minister's Cancer Patient Fund, affordable drugs (Jan Aushadhi, AMRIT pharmacies); (4) palliative care — pain relief (oral morphine availability — NDPS amendment 2014), psychosocial support, home-based care (National Programme for Palliative Care 2012); (5) surveillance and research — population-based and hospital-based cancer registries (ICMR-NCDIR National Cancer Registry Programme, 1981), cancer atlas, NCD portal. History: National Cancer Control Programme (1975) → merged into NPCDCS (2010) → NP-NCD (2023).",
[["Common cancers in India",[["Males","Females"],["Lip and oral cavity","Breast"],["Lung","Cervix"],["Oesophagus","Ovary"],["Stomach","Oral cavity"],["Colorectum","Corpus uteri"]]],
["Cancer control",[["Strategy","Measures"],["Primary prevention","Tobacco control (COTPA, NTCP), HPV and Hep B vaccines, diet, activity, alcohol control"],["Screening (≥ 30 yr)","Oral — OVE; breast — CBE; cervix — VIA/HPV (5-yearly)"],["Treatment","District day-care chemo, SCIs, TCCCs, National Cancer Grid, PMJAY"],["Palliative care","Oral morphine, NPPC, home care"],["Surveillance","PBCR/HBCR (NCRP, ICMR-NCDIR)"]]],
["Cervical cancer elimination (WHO 90-70-90)",["90% girls HPV-vaccinated by age 15","70% women screened by 35 and 45 years","90% of identified lesions treated"]]],
{c:"Cancer burden in India is dominated by tobacco-related, breast and cervical cancers; primary prevention through tobacco control and vaccination, population screening for oral, breast and cervical cancers under NP-NCD, accessible treatment and palliative care form the control strategy."});

Z("cm5",3,"cm",["Obesity is excessive fat accumulation that impairs health, measured by body mass index (BMI = weight kg / height m²); for Asians, BMI ≥ 23 is overweight and ≥ 25 is obese (WHO global: ≥ 25 overweight, ≥ 30 obese); central obesity (waist circumference) is a strong cardiometabolic risk marker.","BMI is a quick screening ruler for body fat; Indians carry more fat around the belly at lower BMI, so lower cut-offs and waist measurement matter.",["BMI classification (WHO): < 18.5 underweight; 18.5–24.9 normal; 25–29.9 pre-obese; 30–34.9 obese I; 35–39.9 II; ≥ 40 III. Asian (WHO 2004/Indian consensus): 18.5–22.9 normal, 23–24.9 overweight, ≥ 25 obese.","Central obesity: waist circumference ≥ 90 cm men, ≥ 80 cm women (Asian Indians); waist–hip ratio > 0.9 men, > 0.85 women; waist–height ratio > 0.5.","Burden: NFHS-5 — 24% women, 23% men overweight/obese; childhood obesity rising; causes — positive energy balance (high-calorie diets, sugar-sweetened beverages, sedentary lifestyle, screen time), genetics, endocrine, drugs, socioeconomic transition.","Prevention: healthy diet, physical activity (150 min/week adults; 60 min/day children), school programmes, food labelling and taxation (12–28% GST on sugary drinks), Eat Right India, Fit India Movement; treatment — diet, exercise, behaviour therapy, drugs (orlistat, GLP-1 RAs), bariatric surgery (BMI ≥ 37.5/32.5 with comorbidity in Asians)."],"Asian BMI: ≥ 23 overweight, ≥ 25 obese; waist ≥ 90 (M) / ≥ 80 (F)."],
"Obesity is defined as an abnormal or excessive fat accumulation that presents a risk to health (WHO); it is a chronic, relapsing, multifactorial disease. Measurement: (1) body mass index (Quetelet's index) = weight (kg)/height (m)² — most widely used, simple, correlates with body fat; limitations — does not distinguish fat from muscle, does not indicate fat distribution, varies with ethnicity and age; (2) waist circumference (measured midway between lower rib margin and iliac crest) — indicator of abdominal (visceral) fat and cardiometabolic risk; cut-offs — WHO: > 94 cm men, > 80 cm women (increased risk), > 102/88 (substantially increased); for Asian Indians: ≥ 90 cm men and ≥ 80 cm women; (3) waist–hip ratio (> 0.90 men, > 0.85 women); (4) waist-to-height ratio > 0.5; (5) skinfold thickness (triceps, subscapular — calipers), bioelectrical impedance, DEXA, CT/MRI (research); (6) in children — BMI-for-age percentiles/Z-scores (WHO growth standards — overweight > +1 SD, obese > +2 SD for 5–19 years; > +2 and > +3 SD for weight-for-height under 5). WHO BMI classification for adults: underweight < 18.5; normal 18.5–24.9; pre-obese (overweight) 25.0–29.9; obese class I 30.0–34.9, class II 35.0–39.9, class III ≥ 40.0. Asian cut-offs (WHO expert consultation 2004; Indian consensus guidelines 2009): normal 18.5–22.9, overweight 23.0–24.9, obese ≥ 25 — because Asian Indians have higher body fat, central obesity and cardiometabolic risk at lower BMI. Burden: globally > 1 billion people obese (2022), adult obesity doubled since 1990; India — NFHS-5 (2019–21): 24% women and 23% men (15–49 years) overweight or obese (BMI ≥ 25), central obesity 40% women and 12% men; childhood overweight 3.4% under-5; rising in urban and rural areas — 'double burden of malnutrition' coexisting with undernutrition. Epidemiological determinants: (1) age (increases up to 50–60), (2) sex (women more), (3) genetic factors (heritability 40–70%; FTO gene, monogenic — leptin deficiency), (4) physical inactivity (sedentary occupations, mechanised transport, screen time), (5) dietary factors — high energy intake, energy-dense processed foods, fast foods, sugar-sweetened beverages, large portion sizes, eating out, (6) socioeconomic status (in developing countries obesity more in affluent but shifting to poor), (7) psychosocial — stress, emotional eating, (8) endocrine (hypothyroidism, Cushing, PCOS), (9) drugs (steroids, antipsychotics, insulin, sulfonylureas, OCPs), (10) sleep deprivation, (11) smoking cessation, (12) early life factors — maternal obesity/diabetes, low birth weight with catch-up growth, formula feeding, (13) obesogenic environment — urbanisation, lack of safe spaces for exercise, food marketing. Health hazards: type 2 diabetes, hypertension, dyslipidaemia, metabolic syndrome, coronary heart disease, stroke, NAFLD, gallstones, osteoarthritis, obstructive sleep apnoea, gout, certain cancers (breast, endometrium, colon, kidney, oesophagus), PCOS and infertility, pregnancy complications, psychological problems, reduced life expectancy. Prevention and control: (1) population approach — healthy food environment (front-of-pack labelling, restricting marketing to children, taxation of sugar-sweetened beverages — 28% GST + cess, trans-fat limits ≤ 2% — FSSAI), Eat Right India and Eat Right School, Fit India Movement (2019), Khelo India, urban planning for active transport, school health and wellness programmes (Ayushman Bharat School Health), workplace wellness, POSHAN Abhiyaan (addresses both under- and over-nutrition), 'Aaj Se Thoda Kam' (10% less oil) campaign; (2) individual approach — dietary modification (calorie deficit of 500–1000 kcal/day, balanced diet rich in fruits, vegetables, whole grains, pulses; limit sugar, salt, fat), physical activity (≥ 150–300 min/week moderate aerobic plus strength training; children 60 min/day), behaviour modification (self-monitoring, goal setting), adequate sleep; target 5–10% weight loss improves comorbidities; (3) pharmacotherapy — orlistat, GLP-1 receptor agonists (liraglutide, semaglutide), tirzepatide, phentermine–topiramate; (4) bariatric/metabolic surgery — for Asian Indians BMI ≥ 37.5 or ≥ 32.5 with comorbidities (sleeve gastrectomy, gastric bypass); (5) screening — BMI and waist circumference measurement during NCD screening at HWCs; World Obesity Day (4 March).",
[["BMI classification",[["Category","WHO (global)","Asian/Indian"],["Underweight","< 18.5","< 18.5"],["Normal","18.5–24.9","18.5–22.9"],["Overweight","25–29.9","23–24.9"],["Obese","≥ 30 (I 30–34.9, II 35–39.9, III ≥ 40)","≥ 25"]]],
["Central obesity cut-offs (Indians)",[["Measure","Men","Women"],["Waist circumference","≥ 90 cm","≥ 80 cm"],["Waist–hip ratio","> 0.90","> 0.85"],["Waist–height ratio","> 0.5","> 0.5"]]],
["Prevention",["Healthy diet; limit sugar, salt, fat, SSBs","Physical activity (150 min/week; children 60 min/day)","Front-of-pack labelling, SSB tax, trans-fat limits","Eat Right India, Fit India Movement, school health","Behaviour change; screening at HWCs"]]],
{c:"Obesity, assessed by BMI with Asian cut-offs and waist circumference, is rising rapidly in India and drives NCDs; population-level food and activity environment changes plus individual lifestyle, drug and surgical therapy form the control approach."});

Z("cm5",4,"cm",["Protein energy malnutrition (PEM), now called undernutrition, results from inadequate intake of energy and protein often worsened by infections; it presents as kwashiorkor, marasmus or marasmic kwashiorkor and is assessed by underweight, stunting and wasting; it is a major cause of child mortality and is addressed by breastfeeding, complementary feeding, ICDS/POSHAN, SAM management (NRCs, CMAM) and infection control.","Malnutrition and infection form a vicious cycle — a hungry child gets sick more often, and a sick child eats less and loses more.",["Burden (NFHS-5): stunting 35.5%, wasting 19.3%, severe wasting 7.7%, underweight 32.1% in under-5s; contributes to ~45% of child deaths globally.","Classification: Gomez (weight-for-age %), IAP (weight-for-age grades I–IV), Waterlow (wasting/stunting), WHO Z-scores (WAZ, HAZ, WHZ < −2 SD moderate, < −3 SD severe); SAM — WHZ < −3, MUAC < 11.5 cm, or bilateral pitting oedema.","Kwashiorkor (protein deficiency, oedema, flaky-paint dermatosis, hair changes, fatty liver, apathy) vs marasmus (severe wasting, 'old man' face, no oedema, alert); causes — poverty, food insecurity, poor feeding practices, infections, low birth weight, maternal illiteracy.","Prevention/control: exclusive breastfeeding 6 months, complementary feeding from 6 months, growth monitoring (MCP card), ICDS supplementary nutrition, POSHAN Abhiyaan, Mission Poshan 2.0, mid-day meal (PM POSHAN), immunisation, deworming, vitamin A, WASH; SAM management — Nutrition Rehabilitation Centres (facility-based for complicated SAM), CMAM with RUTF (community-based for uncomplicated SAM), WHO 10 steps."],"SAM: WHZ < −3 SD or MUAC < 11.5 cm or bilateral oedema."],
"Protein energy malnutrition (PEM) — WHO now prefers 'undernutrition' — is a range of pathological conditions arising from coincident lack of varying proportions of protein and calories, occurring most frequently in infants and young children and commonly associated with infections. Burden: globally 14.8 crore children under 5 stunted, 4.5 crore wasted (2022); undernutrition is an underlying factor in ~45% of under-five deaths; India — NFHS-5 (2019–21): stunting (low height-for-age) 35.5%, wasting (low weight-for-height) 19.3%, severe wasting 7.7%, underweight (low weight-for-age) 32.1%, overweight 3.4%; anaemia 67% (6–59 months); highest in Bihar, Jharkhand, Meghalaya, Gujarat, Maharashtra; long-term consequences — impaired cognitive development, reduced school performance and productivity, increased adult NCD risk; intergenerational cycle (malnourished mothers → low birth weight babies). Aetiology and risk factors: (1) immediate causes — inadequate dietary intake (quantity, quality, frequency, energy density) and disease (infections — diarrhoea, measles, ARI, TB, worms, HIV — malnutrition–infection cycle: infection causes anorexia, malabsorption, catabolism, increased requirements; malnutrition impairs immunity); (2) underlying causes — household food insecurity, inadequate maternal and child care practices (late initiation and lack of exclusive breastfeeding, early or delayed and inappropriate complementary feeding, poor hygiene, food taboos), poor water, sanitation and health services; (3) basic causes — poverty, illiteracy (especially maternal), gender inequality, large family size, short birth spacing, early marriage, social and political factors, low birth weight. Clinical forms: (a) kwashiorkor (Cicely Williams, 1935; 'disease of the deposed child' — after weaning) — predominant protein deficiency with relatively adequate calories (also attributed to oxidative stress, aflatoxins); age 1–3 years; features — growth failure, bilateral pitting oedema (starting in feet), muscle wasting masked by oedema, psychomotor changes (apathy, irritability, misery), skin changes (flaky-paint dermatosis, hyperpigmentation, desquamation), hair changes (sparse, thin, easily pluckable, depigmented — 'flag sign'), moon face, hepatomegaly (fatty liver), anaemia, diarrhoea, hypoalbuminaemia, signs of vitamin deficiencies; (b) nutritional marasmus — chronic severe deficiency of both energy and protein (starvation); usually < 1 year (early weaning, dilute formula, repeated diarrhoea); features — severe growth retardation and wasting of muscle and subcutaneous fat ('skin and bones'), 'old man' or 'monkey' face, no oedema, alert, hungry, wrinkled loose skin (especially buttocks — 'baggy pants'), hair usually normal, diarrhoea; (c) marasmic kwashiorkor — features of both (severe wasting with oedema). Classification: (1) Gomez classification (weight-for-age as % of reference median: grade I 75–90%, II 60–75%, III < 60%); (2) IAP classification (weight-for-age: normal > 80%, grade I 71–80%, II 61–70%, III 51–60%, IV ≤ 50%; suffix K for oedema); (3) Waterlow classification (wasting — weight-for-height; stunting — height-for-age); (4) Wellcome Trust classification (weight-for-age with or without oedema: underweight 60–80% without oedema, kwashiorkor 60–80% with oedema, marasmus < 60% without oedema, marasmic kwashiorkor < 60% with oedema); (5) WHO classification (Z-scores based on WHO Child Growth Standards 2006): underweight (weight-for-age < −2 SD; severe < −3 SD), stunting (height/length-for-age < −2 SD — chronic undernutrition), wasting (weight-for-height < −2 SD — acute undernutrition); moderate acute malnutrition (MAM) — WHZ between −2 and −3 SD or MUAC 11.5–12.5 cm; severe acute malnutrition (SAM, 6–59 months) — WHZ < −3 SD, or MUAC < 11.5 cm, or presence of bilateral pitting oedema; (6) MUAC (mid-upper arm circumference; 1–5 years: > 13.5 cm normal, 12.5–13.5 borderline, < 12.5 malnourished; Shakir's tape — green/yellow/red). Prevention and control: (1) health promotion — nutrition education on breastfeeding (early initiation within 1 hour, exclusive for 6 months — MAA programme), complementary feeding from 6 months (energy-dense, diverse, frequent, hygienic), maternal nutrition (IFA, calcium, supplementary food, Pradhan Mantri Matru Vandana Yojana), family planning and birth spacing, female education, food security (National Food Security Act 2013 — PDS, PM Garib Kalyan Anna Yojana), improving household income, WASH (Swachh Bharat, Jal Jeevan Mission); (2) specific protection — supplementary nutrition through Integrated Child Development Services (anganwadi — take-home rations/hot cooked meals), PM POSHAN (mid-day meal) for school children, fortified foods (rice, wheat flour, oil, salt), immunisation (measles), vitamin A supplementation, deworming (National Deworming Day — albendazole biannual), IFA (Anaemia Mukt Bharat), zinc and ORS for diarrhoea, POSHAN Abhiyaan (2018; National Nutrition Mission — convergence, ICT-real time monitoring through Poshan Tracker, community-based events, Jan Andolan, Poshan Maah in September) and Mission Saksham Anganwadi & Poshan 2.0 (2021); (3) early diagnosis and treatment — growth monitoring and promotion (monthly weighing and plotting on growth chart in MCP card at anganwadi — early detection of growth faltering), MUAC screening by ASHA/AWW, IMNCI, Village Health Sanitation and Nutrition Days; management of SAM — facility-based management at Nutrition Rehabilitation Centres (NRCs; for SAM with medical complications, failed appetite test or < 6 months) following WHO 10 steps (treat/prevent hypoglycaemia, hypothermia, dehydration (ReSoMal), correct electrolyte imbalance, treat infection (antibiotics), correct micronutrient deficiencies, start cautious feeding (F-75), achieve catch-up growth (F-100/RUTF), provide sensory stimulation and emotional support, prepare for follow-up after recovery) — stabilisation then rehabilitation phase; community-based management of acute malnutrition (CMAM) for uncomplicated SAM with appetite — ready-to-use therapeutic food (RUTF)/locally prepared energy-dense foods at home with weekly follow-up; discharge criteria (WHZ ≥ −2, MUAC ≥ 12.5, no oedema); (4) rehabilitation — follow-up, nutrition counselling, linkage with ICDS and social protection.",
[["WHO classification (Z-scores)",[["Indicator","Moderate","Severe","Reflects"],["Weight-for-age (underweight)","< −2 SD","< −3 SD","Composite"],["Height-for-age (stunting)","< −2 SD","< −3 SD","Chronic"],["Weight-for-height (wasting)","< −2 SD","< −3 SD","Acute"],["MUAC (6–59 months)","11.5–12.5 cm (MAM)","< 11.5 cm (SAM)","Acute"],["Bilateral pitting oedema","—","SAM","Kwashiorkor"]]],
["Kwashiorkor vs marasmus",[["Feature","Kwashiorkor","Marasmus"],["Deficiency","Mainly protein","Energy + protein"],["Age","1–3 years","< 1 year"],["Oedema","Present","Absent"],["Wasting","Masked by oedema","Severe, 'skin and bones'"],["Mental state","Apathetic, irritable","Alert, hungry"],["Skin","Flaky-paint dermatosis","Loose, wrinkled"],["Hair","Sparse, depigmented, flag sign","Usually normal"],["Liver","Fatty, enlarged","Normal"],["Serum albumin","Low","Near normal"]]],
["Classifications",[["System","Basis"],["Gomez","Weight-for-age % (I 75–90, II 60–75, III < 60)"],["IAP","Weight-for-age % (I–IV)"],["Waterlow","Wasting (W/H), stunting (H/A)"],["Wellcome Trust","Weight-for-age + oedema"],["WHO","Z-scores, MUAC, oedema"]]],
["Control measures",[["Level","Interventions"],["Promotion","Breastfeeding, complementary feeding, maternal nutrition, WASH, NFSA"],["Specific protection","ICDS, PM POSHAN, POSHAN Abhiyaan, fortification, vitamin A, deworming, immunisation"],["Early detection","Growth monitoring (MCP card), MUAC screening, IMNCI"],["Treatment","NRC (WHO 10 steps, F-75/F-100), CMAM with RUTF"],["Rehabilitation","Follow-up, counselling, social protection"]]]],
{i:"Child undernutrition in India remains high, contributing to mortality, impaired development and the intergenerational cycle of malnutrition.",x:[["WHO 10 steps for SAM",["Hypoglycaemia","Hypothermia","Dehydration (ReSoMal)","Electrolytes","Infection","Micronutrients","Cautious feeding (F-75)","Catch-up growth (F-100/RUTF)","Sensory stimulation","Follow-up"]]],f:"Poverty, food insecurity, poor feeding, LBW, illiteracy → inadequate intake ⇄ infection (vicious cycle) → PEM (kwashiorkor/marasmus) → stunting, wasting, death\n Interventions: breastfeeding, ICDS/POSHAN, growth monitoring, SAM care (NRC/CMAM), WASH",c:"Protein energy malnutrition arises from inadequate diet and infection rooted in poverty and poor care practices; it is classified by WHO Z-scores and MUAC, and is tackled through breastfeeding and complementary feeding, ICDS/POSHAN, growth monitoring, infection control and facility- and community-based SAM management."});

Z("cm5",5,"cm",["Micronutrient deficiencies ('hidden hunger') of vitamin A, iodine and iron are major public health problems in India causing blindness, goitre and brain damage, and anaemia respectively; they are tackled by supplementation (vitamin A prophylaxis, IFA under Anaemia Mukt Bharat), fortification (iodised salt, double-fortified salt, fortified rice) and dietary diversification.","Hidden hunger: a child may look well-fed but lack vitamin A (eyes), iodine (brain/thyroid) or iron (blood) — small amounts with huge impacts.",["Vitamin A deficiency: night blindness, xerophthalmia (X1A conjunctival xerosis, X1B Bitot's spots, X2 corneal xerosis, X3 ulceration/keratomalacia, XN, XS, XF); ↑ child mortality; prophylaxis — 1 lakh IU at 9 months, 2 lakh IU every 6 months to 5 years (9 doses); treatment — 2 lakh IU days 0, 1, 14.","Iodine deficiency disorders: goitre, cretinism, mental retardation, deaf-mutism, stillbirths, hypothyroidism; indicators — total goitre rate (> 5% = public health problem), urinary iodine (median 100–199 µg/L adequate), neonatal TSH; NIDDCP (1992; NGCP 1962) — universal salt iodisation (≥ 30 ppm at production, ≥ 15 ppm at consumer), ban on non-iodised salt, monitoring (STK), Global IDD Prevention Day 21 October.","Iron deficiency anaemia: NFHS-5 — 67% children, 57% women, 52% pregnant women anaemic; Hb cut-offs (WHO) — children 6–59 m < 11, women < 12, pregnant < 11, men < 13 g/dL.","Anaemia Mukt Bharat (6×6×6): prophylactic IFA (biweekly syrup 6–59 m; weekly 5–9 yr, adolescents WIFS; daily 180 days pregnancy/lactation), deworming, behaviour change, test and treat (digital Hb), fortified foods, address non-nutritional causes (malaria, haemoglobinopathies, fluorosis)."],"Iodised salt: ≥ 30 ppm at production, ≥ 15 ppm at consumer level."],
"Micronutrients (vitamins and minerals) are required in minute amounts but are essential for growth, development, immunity and metabolism; deficiencies affect over 2 billion people globally ('hidden hunger'). Vitamin A deficiency (VAD): causes — inadequate dietary intake of retinol (animal foods) and β-carotene (green leafy vegetables, yellow fruits), fat malabsorption, infections (measles, diarrhoea, respiratory infections increase requirement and loss), PEM, early weaning; vulnerable — children 6 months–6 years, pregnant and lactating women; manifestations — night blindness (earliest; impaired dark adaptation — rhodopsin), xerophthalmia (WHO classification 1982): XN — night blindness, X1A — conjunctival xerosis, X1B — Bitot's spots (foamy triangular grey-white patches on bulbar conjunctiva), X2 — corneal xerosis, X3A — corneal ulceration/keratomalacia < 1/3 corneal surface, X3B — ≥ 1/3, XS — corneal scar, XF — xerophthalmic fundus; extra-ocular — follicular hyperkeratosis, growth retardation, anaemia, increased severity and mortality from infections (VAD increases child mortality by ~23%; supplementation reduces all-cause mortality 12–24%); public health criteria (WHO) — XN > 1%, X1B > 0.5%, X2+X3 > 0.01%, XS > 0.05% in children < 6 years, serum retinol < 10 µg/dL > 5%; status in India — clinical VAD has declined markedly (Bitot's spots < 1%) but subclinical deficiency persists (~18% CNNS 2016–18); control — (1) short-term: periodic high-dose vitamin A supplementation under National Prophylaxis Programme against Nutritional Blindness (1970; now part of child health) — 1,00,000 IU at 9 months (with MR vaccine) and 2,00,000 IU every 6 months from 16–18 months up to 5 years (total 9 doses; biannual rounds), treatment of xerophthalmia (2,00,000 IU on day 0, day 1 and after 2 weeks; half dose 6–12 months, quarter < 6 months), vitamin A in measles; (2) medium-term: fortification (edible oil, milk — FSSAI +F standards); (3) long-term: dietary diversification — promotion of green leafy vegetables, yellow-orange fruits, kitchen gardens, breastfeeding, nutrition education; control of infections (measles immunisation, diarrhoea control). Iodine deficiency disorders (IDD): iodine is essential for synthesis of thyroid hormones (T3, T4) required for growth and brain development; requirement 150 µg/day adults, 250 µg in pregnancy and lactation, 90–120 µg in children; deficiency due to low iodine in soil and water (Himalayan and sub-Himalayan belt, flood plains, hilly areas — now recognised throughout India), goitrogens (cassava, cabbage family, millet, thiocyanates); spectrum of IDD (Hetzel) — fetus: abortions, stillbirths, congenital anomalies, perinatal mortality, endemic cretinism (neurological — mental retardation, deaf-mutism, spastic diplegia, squint; myxoedematous — hypothyroidism, dwarfism), psychomotor defects; neonate: neonatal goitre, neonatal hypothyroidism; child and adolescent: goitre, juvenile hypothyroidism, impaired mental function (~13.5 IQ points lower), retarded physical development; adult: goitre with complications, hypothyroidism, impaired mental function, iodine-induced hyperthyroidism; India — of 414 districts surveyed, 337 endemic (TGR > 10%); estimated 7 crore with goitre historically; indicators (WHO/UNICEF/ICCIDD) — total goitre rate in school children 6–12 years (≥ 5% indicates public health problem; mild 5–19.9%, moderate 20–29.9%, severe ≥ 30%), median urinary iodine concentration (adequate 100–199 µg/L in children; < 20 severe, 20–49 moderate, 50–99 mild deficiency; 150–249 in pregnancy), neonatal TSH (> 5 mU/L in > 3% — deficiency), proportion of households consuming adequately iodised salt (> 90% target; India ~94% — NFHS-5); control — National Goitre Control Programme (1962) → National Iodine Deficiency Disorders Control Programme (NIDDCP, 1992) with universal salt iodisation (all edible salt iodised; ban on sale of non-iodised salt for human consumption under Food Safety and Standards Act 2006/Prevention of Food Adulteration Act — since 2005 nationwide), iodine content ≥ 30 ppm at production and ≥ 15 ppm at consumer level, potassium iodate as fortificant (more stable than iodide), double fortified salt (iodine + iron), surveys (district IDD surveys every 5 years), laboratory monitoring of salt and urinary iodine (salt testing kits — STK used by ASHAs), IEC, Global IDD Prevention Day (21 October); iodised oil injection/capsules in severe areas (historical). Nutritional anaemia (iron deficiency anaemia): anaemia defined by WHO haemoglobin cut-offs — children 6–59 months < 11 g/dL, children 5–11 years < 11.5, adolescents 12–14 years and non-pregnant women ≥ 15 years < 12, pregnant women < 11, men ≥ 15 years < 13; severity — mild, moderate (7–9.9 in pregnancy/children), severe (< 7 g/dL); burden in India (NFHS-5): children 6–59 months 67.1%, non-pregnant women 15–49 years 57.2%, pregnant women 52.2%, adolescent girls 59.1%, adolescent boys 31.1%, men 25%; causes — inadequate dietary iron (vegetarian diets, low bioavailability of non-haem iron, phytates, tannins in tea), increased requirements (pregnancy, growth), blood loss (hookworm, menstruation, malaria, GI bleed), folate and B12 deficiency, infections and inflammation, haemoglobinopathies, closely spaced pregnancies; consequences — increased maternal mortality, low birth weight, preterm birth, impaired cognitive and motor development, reduced work capacity and productivity, increased infections; control — National Nutritional Anaemia Prophylaxis Programme (1970) → National Iron Plus Initiative (2013) → Anaemia Mukt Bharat (AMB, 2018; under POSHAN Abhiyaan) — '6×6×6 strategy': six target beneficiary groups (children 6–59 months, 5–9 years, adolescents 10–19 years, women of reproductive age 20–24, pregnant women, lactating mothers), six interventions — (1) prophylactic iron–folic acid supplementation (children 6–59 months: 1 mL IFA syrup (20 mg iron + 100 µg folic acid) biweekly; 5–9 years: 45 mg iron + 400 µg FA pink tablet weekly; adolescents 10–19 years: 60 mg iron + 500 µg FA blue tablet weekly — WIFS (Weekly Iron and Folic Acid Supplementation) in schools and anganwadis; women of reproductive age: 60 mg + 500 µg weekly; pregnant women: 60 mg + 500 µg daily for 180 days from second trimester; lactating: 180 days postpartum), (2) periodic deworming (albendazole biannually — National Deworming Day 10 February and 10 August), (3) intensified year-round behaviour change communication (Solution campaign — delayed cord clamping, diet diversity, iron-rich foods), (4) testing and treatment of anaemia using digital invasive haemoglobinometers (point-of-care) — treatment with therapeutic IFA/IV iron sucrose/FCM for moderate–severe anaemia in pregnancy, (5) mandatory provision of IFA-fortified foods in government-funded health programmes (ICDS, PM POSHAN, PDS — fortified rice rollout 2024), (6) addressing non-nutritional causes of anaemia in endemic pockets (malaria, haemoglobinopathies — sickle cell anaemia elimination mission 2023, fluorosis); six institutional mechanisms (intra-ministerial coordination, national anaemia mukt bharat unit, national centre of excellence and advanced research on anaemia control, convergence, supply chain, AMB dashboard/report card); targets — reduce anaemia prevalence by 3 percentage points per year.",
[["Xerophthalmia (WHO)",[["Grade","Sign"],["XN","Night blindness"],["X1A","Conjunctival xerosis"],["X1B","Bitot's spots"],["X2","Corneal xerosis"],["X3A","Corneal ulcer/keratomalacia < 1/3"],["X3B","≥ 1/3 cornea"],["XS","Corneal scar"],["XF","Xerophthalmic fundus"]]],
["Spectrum of IDD",[["Stage","Effects"],["Fetus","Abortion, stillbirth, cretinism, anomalies"],["Neonate","Neonatal hypothyroidism, goitre"],["Child","Goitre, impaired mental and physical development"],["Adult","Goitre, hypothyroidism, impaired mental function"]]],
["IDD indicators",[["Indicator","Public health problem"],["Total goitre rate (6–12 yr)","≥ 5%"],["Median urinary iodine","< 100 µg/L"],["Neonatal TSH > 5 mU/L","> 3%"],["Households with adequately iodised salt","Target > 90%"]]],
["Anaemia Mukt Bharat IFA dosing",[["Group","Dose and frequency"],["6–59 months","1 mL syrup (20 mg Fe + 100 µg FA) biweekly"],["5–9 years","45 mg Fe + 400 µg FA weekly"],["10–19 years (WIFS)","60 mg Fe + 500 µg FA weekly"],["Women of reproductive age","60 mg + 500 µg weekly"],["Pregnant / lactating","60 mg + 500 µg daily × 180 days"]]]],
{i:"Micronutrient deficiencies of vitamin A, iodine and iron remain significant in India despite progress; programmes combine supplementation, fortification and dietary change.",x:[["Anaemia Mukt Bharat 6 interventions",["Prophylactic IFA","Deworming","BCC campaign","Digital Hb test and treat","Fortified foods in public programmes","Address non-nutritional causes"]]],f:"Hidden hunger:\n Vitamin A ↓ → night blindness → xerophthalmia → keratomalacia; ↑ mortality → prophylaxis 9 doses\n Iodine ↓ → goitre, cretinism, ↓ IQ → universal salt iodisation (30/15 ppm)\n Iron ↓ → anaemia → AMB 6×6×6",c:"Vitamin A deficiency (xerophthalmia), iodine deficiency disorders and iron deficiency anaemia are controlled through biannual vitamin A prophylaxis, universal salt iodisation under NIDDCP, and the Anaemia Mukt Bharat 6×6×6 strategy, supported by fortification and dietary diversification."});

Z("cm5",6,"cm",["Food fortification is the addition of micronutrients to commonly eaten foods (salt, oil, milk, rice, wheat) to prevent deficiencies; nutrition programmes like ICDS (anganwadi services), PM POSHAN (mid-day meal) and POSHAN Abhiyaan deliver supplementary nutrition, counselling and monitoring to children, adolescent girls and mothers.","Fortification quietly improves nutrition without changing eating habits; programmes provide food, check growth and teach families.",["Fortification (FSSAI 2018, '+F' logo): iodised salt, double-fortified salt (iodine + iron), oil and milk (vitamins A, D), wheat flour and rice (iron, folic acid, B12) — fortified rice in PDS/ICDS/PM POSHAN (2024); advantages — cost-effective, no behaviour change, reaches large populations.","ICDS (1975; Mission Saksham Anganwadi & Poshan 2.0): six services — supplementary nutrition, pre-school education, nutrition & health education, immunisation, health check-up, referral; beneficiaries — children 6 m–6 yr, pregnant and lactating women, adolescent girls (14–18); SNP norms — 500 kcal + 12–15 g protein (children), 800 kcal + 20–25 g (SAM), 600 kcal + 18–20 g (pregnant/lactating).","PM POSHAN (formerly Mid-Day Meal 1995): hot cooked meal in government schools — primary 450 kcal + 12 g protein, upper primary 700 kcal + 20 g protein; improves enrolment, attendance, nutrition.","POSHAN Abhiyaan (2018, National Nutrition Mission): targets ↓ stunting, undernutrition, LBW by 2%/year and anaemia by 3%/year; convergence, ICT (Poshan Tracker), Jan Andolan, Poshan Maah (September), Anaemia Mukt Bharat; others — NFSA 2013, PMMVY, Take Home Ration, Kishori scheme."],"ICDS six services: 'SNP, PSE, NHE, Immunisation, Health check-up, Referral'."],
"Food fortification: the practice of deliberately increasing the content of essential micronutrients in a food to improve its nutritional quality and provide public health benefit with minimal risk (WHO/FAO); types — mass (large-scale) fortification of staple foods consumed by the general population (salt, wheat flour, rice, edible oil, milk), targeted fortification (complementary foods for infants, foods for school feeding, supplementary foods), market-driven, biofortification (breeding crops with higher micronutrient content — iron-rich pearl millet, zinc wheat, vitamin A maize), point-of-use (home) fortification (micronutrient powders sprinkled on food); requirements — fortificant must be safe, bioavailable, stable, not alter taste/colour/shelf life, vehicle consumed regularly in adequate amounts by target population, centrally processed, affordable, with quality control and monitoring; examples in India — iodised salt (universal since 2005; potassium iodate 30 ppm at production), double fortified salt (iodine + iron — developed by NIN Hyderabad; used in PM POSHAN/ICDS in several states), fortified edible oil (vitamins A and D — mandatory under FSSAI 2021), fortified milk (vitamins A and D), fortified wheat flour (atta — iron, folic acid, vitamin B12), fortified rice (fortified rice kernels blended 1:100 — iron, folic acid, B12; rolled out in all government schemes — PDS, ICDS, PM POSHAN — nationwide by 2024), vitamin D and A in vanaspati; Food Safety and Standards (Fortification of Foods) Regulations 2018 with '+F' logo; advantages — cost-effective, reaches large population without changing dietary habits, sustainable, socially acceptable; limitations — may not reach the poorest who consume unprocessed foods, risk of excess in some (e.g., iron in thalassaemia/sickle cell — debated), monitoring and quality control challenges. Integrated Child Development Services (ICDS): launched on 2 October 1975 (33 blocks; universalised); world's largest early childhood care programme; under Ministry of Women and Child Development; delivered through anganwadi centres (one per ~1000 population in rural/urban, 400–800 in tribal/hilly; mini-AWC for 150–400) by anganwadi worker (AWW) and helper; restructured as Mission Saksham Anganwadi and Poshan 2.0 (2021); objectives — improve nutritional and health status of children 0–6 years, lay foundation for psychological, physical and social development, reduce mortality, morbidity, malnutrition and school dropout, coordinate policy and implementation among departments, enhance mothers' capability to look after normal health and nutritional needs of children; beneficiaries — children 6 months–6 years, pregnant women, lactating mothers, adolescent girls 14–18 years (Scheme for Adolescent Girls in aspirational districts and NE); six services — (1) supplementary nutrition (SNP) — take-home rations (children 6 months–3 years, pregnant and lactating women) and hot cooked meal plus morning snack (children 3–6 years) for 300 days/year; revised nutritional norms (2017/Poshan 2.0): children 6 months–6 years — 500 kcal and 12–15 g protein per day; severely underweight/SAM children — 800 kcal and 20–25 g protein; pregnant and lactating women — 600 kcal and 18–20 g protein; adolescent girls — 600 kcal and 18–20 g protein; use of millets and fortified foods; (2) pre-school non-formal education (3–6 years) — early childhood care and education; (3) nutrition and health education (women 15–45 years); (4) immunisation (with health system); (5) health check-up (antenatal and postnatal care, child check-ups, growth monitoring — by ANM/MO); (6) referral services; services 1, 2 and 3 provided by AWC; 4, 5, 6 by health department in convergence (Village Health Sanitation and Nutrition Day); growth monitoring using WHO growth charts and Poshan Tracker app (real-time monitoring). PM POSHAN (Pradhan Mantri Poshan Shakti Nirman, formerly National Programme of Nutritional Support to Primary Education — Mid-Day Meal Scheme, 1995; Tamil Nadu pioneered in 1960s; Supreme Court order 2001 for cooked meals): covers children in classes I–VIII in government and government-aided schools (and Balvatika/pre-primary) — about 11–12 crore children; hot cooked meal on all school days; nutritional norms — primary (I–V): 450 kcal and 12 g protein; upper primary (VI–VIII): 700 kcal and 20 g protein; with food grains 100 g/150 g, pulses 20/30 g, vegetables 50/75 g, oil 5/7.5 g; micronutrient supplementation (IFA, vitamin A, deworming tablets) through school health; School Nutrition Gardens, tithi bhojan (community contributions), social audit; objectives — improve nutritional status, enrolment, attendance and retention, socialisation (caste barriers), gender equity, and employment for women (cooks). POSHAN Abhiyaan (Prime Minister's Overarching Scheme for Holistic Nourishment — National Nutrition Mission, launched 8 March 2018, Jhunjhunu): goal — reduce stunting, undernutrition and low birth weight by 2% per year and anaemia (children, adolescents, women) by 3% per year; target stunting 25% by 2022 ('Mission 25 by 2022'); four pillars — technology (ICT-Real Time Monitoring — Poshan Tracker app for anganwadi workers, growth measuring devices), convergence (ICDS, NHM, PM POSHAN, Swachh Bharat, drinking water, PMMVY, NRLM — convergence action plans at state, district, block), behaviour change (Jan Andolan — community-based events, Poshan Maah in September and Poshan Pakhwada in March), capacity building (Incremental Learning Approach for frontline workers); includes Anaemia Mukt Bharat, Mission Indradhanush linkages, management of SAM (community-based), first 1000 days focus; Mission Poshan 2.0 merges ICDS, POSHAN Abhiyaan and Scheme for Adolescent Girls. Other nutrition programmes: National Food Security Act 2013 (legal entitlement — 5 kg food grains/person/month at subsidised prices to 75% rural and 50% urban; Antyodaya Anna Yojana 35 kg/household; free under PMGKAY since 2023; maternity benefit; meals for children), Pradhan Mantri Matru Vandana Yojana (₹5000 conditional cash transfer for first child; ₹6000 for second girl child), Vitamin A prophylaxis, NIDDCP, Mothers' Absolute Affection (MAA) for breastfeeding, Home-Based Newborn and Young Child Care, Eat Right India, millet promotion (International Year of Millets 2023).",
[["Fortified foods in India",[["Food","Fortificants","Programme"],["Salt","Iodine (± iron — DFS)","Universal; DFS in ICDS/PM POSHAN"],["Edible oil","Vitamins A, D","Mandatory (FSSAI)"],["Milk","Vitamins A, D","Voluntary/dairy co-ops"],["Rice","Iron, folic acid, B12","PDS, ICDS, PM POSHAN (2024)"],["Wheat flour","Iron, folic acid, B12","Public programmes"]]],
["ICDS supplementary nutrition norms",[["Beneficiary","Energy","Protein"],["Children 6 m–6 yr","500 kcal","12–15 g"],["Severely underweight/SAM","800 kcal","20–25 g"],["Pregnant & lactating women","600 kcal","18–20 g"],["Adolescent girls","600 kcal","18–20 g"]]],
["PM POSHAN norms",[["Level","Energy","Protein"],["Primary (I–V)","450 kcal","12 g"],["Upper primary (VI–VIII)","700 kcal","20 g"]]],
["POSHAN Abhiyaan",["Targets: ↓ stunting, undernutrition, LBW 2%/yr; anaemia 3%/yr","Pillars: ICT (Poshan Tracker), convergence, Jan Andolan, capacity building","Poshan Maah (September)","Mission Saksham Anganwadi & Poshan 2.0 (2021)"]]],
{c:"Food fortification of staples and nutrition programmes — ICDS with its six anganwadi services, PM POSHAN school meals and the convergent, technology-driven POSHAN Abhiyaan — together form India's strategy against undernutrition and micronutrient deficiencies."});

Z("cm6",0,"cm",["Water purification makes water safe for drinking; on a large scale it involves storage, filtration (slow sand or rapid sand filters) and disinfection (chlorination), while on a small (household) scale it uses boiling, chemical disinfection (chlorine tablets, bleaching powder), filtration (candle filters) and other methods; wells are disinfected with bleaching powder.","Large-scale purification copies nature — let water settle (storage), pass it through sand (filtration), then kill remaining germs (chlorine).",["Safe and wholesome water: free from pathogens, harmful chemicals, pleasant to taste, usable for domestic purposes; standards — BIS 10500 (coliforms nil in 100 mL).","Large scale: (1) storage — sedimentation, 90% bacteria die in 5–7 days, ↓ turbidity; (2) filtration — slow sand (biological) filter: vital layer (schmutzdecke/zoogleal layer), 0.1–0.4 m³/m²/h, 99.9% bacteria removal; rapid sand (mechanical) filter: coagulation with alum, flocculation, sedimentation, backwashing, 5–15 m³/m²/h; (3) disinfection — chlorination (residual 0.5 mg/L after 1 h contact), ozone, UV.","Small scale (household): boiling (rolling boil 1 min — best), chlorine tablets (halazone 4 mg/L; 0.5 g chlorine tablet for 20 L), bleaching powder (33% available chlorine), iodine (2 drops of 2% tincture/L), potassium permanganate (not recommended), filtration (ceramic candle — Pasteur–Chamberland, Berkefeld), RO, UV, SODIS (solar), alum.","Disinfection of wells: calculate volume (3.14 × d² × h × 1000/4 litres), bleaching powder 2.5 g per 1000 L for 1 ppm (orthotoluidine test for residual 0.5 mg/L after 1 h); 'double pot' method for continuous chlorination."],"Slow sand = biological (vital layer); rapid sand = mechanical (alum + backwash)."],
"Safe and wholesome water is defined as water that is free from pathogenic agents, free from harmful chemical substances, pleasant to the taste (free from colour and odour), and usable for domestic purposes. Requirements — WHO recommends 150–200 litres per capita per day in urban areas; Jal Jeevan Mission — 55 lpcd for rural households (tap connections). Water-related diseases: water-borne (cholera, typhoid, hepatitis A and E, polio, diarrhoeas, dysentery), water-washed (scabies, trachoma, skin infections — inadequate water for hygiene), water-based (dracunculiasis, schistosomiasis), water-related vector-borne (malaria, dengue, filaria, JE), chemical (fluorosis, arsenicosis, nitrates — methaemoglobinaemia). Drinking water quality standards: BIS IS 10500:2012 and WHO guidelines — microbiological: E. coli/thermotolerant coliforms and total coliforms must not be detectable in any 100 mL sample; physical — turbidity < 1 NTU (permissible 5), colour, odour, taste acceptable; chemical — pH 6.5–8.5, total dissolved solids < 500 mg/L (permissible 2000), fluoride 1.0 mg/L (permissible 1.5), nitrate 45 mg/L, arsenic 0.01 mg/L, lead 0.01, iron 0.3, hardness 200 mg/L, chlorides 250 mg/L, residual free chlorine 0.2 mg/L minimum at consumer end; bacteriological indicators — coliforms (E. coli — specific indicator of faecal pollution), faecal streptococci, Clostridium perfringens (remote pollution); tests — presumptive coliform test (multiple tube/MPN), membrane filtration, H2S strip test (field). Purification of water on a large scale (municipal water works) — components: (1) storage — water from river or source pumped into natural or artificial reservoirs; purification occurs by physical (sedimentation — 90% of suspended impurities settle in 24 h; turbidity reduced; water becomes clearer), chemical (aerobic bacteria oxidise organic matter; free ammonia reduced, nitrates increased) and biological changes (pathogens die — total bacterial count falls by up to 90% in first 5–7 days; 'storage is natural purification'); optimum storage period 10–14 days (longer storage allows algal growth); (2) filtration — second stage; removes 98–99% bacteria and impurities; two types: (a) slow sand filter (biological filter; English filter, first used in Scotland 1804, London 1829) — elements: supernatant (raw) water (1–1.5 m depth; constant level; provides head of water; waiting period of 3–12 h for sedimentation and oxidation), bed of graded sand (1 m depth, effective size 0.2–0.3 mm), under-drainage system (gravel and porous pipes), filter control valves (venturi meter, regulating valve to maintain constant rate of filtration — 0.1–0.4 m³/m²/h); vital layer (Schmutzdecke, zoogleal layer, filter skin) — slimy, gelatinous layer of algae, plankton, diatoms and bacteria forming on surface of sand bed in a few days ('ripening of filter') — the 'heart' of the slow sand filter; removes organic matter, holds bacteria and oxidises ammoniacal nitrogen into nitrates; removes 99.9% bacteria; when the filter bed becomes clogged (resistance increases), top 1–2 cm of sand is scraped off ('cleaning'), every few weeks; re-sanding after several scrapings; advantages — simple construction and operation, cheap, high physical, chemical and bacteriological quality, less skilled labour; disadvantages — large area required, slow, costly cleaning (labour), not suitable for highly turbid water; (b) rapid sand filter (mechanical filter; American filter, first in USA 1885) — steps: coagulation (addition of chemical coagulant — alum (aluminium sulphate) 5–40 mg/L — forms thick white flocculent precipitate of aluminium hydroxide), rapid mixing (flash mixer — few minutes), flocculation (slow gentle stirring for ~30 min in flocculation chamber — larger flocs formed), sedimentation (clarifier — 2–6 h; most flocs with bacteria settle), filtration through rapid sand filter bed (sand 1 m depth, effective size 0.4–0.7 mm over gravel; rate 5–15 m³/m²/h — 40–50 times faster than slow sand filters; alum floc not removed by sedimentation forms a layer on top which acts like vital layer and holds back bacteria); backwashing (when filter clogged, reversing flow of water and compressed air to wash sand — every 24 h or less); removes 98–99% bacteria; advantages — requires less space, rapid, can deal with raw turbid water, cleaning by backwashing easy, less loss of head; disadvantages — skilled operation, costly, bacterial removal slightly less than slow sand filter; (3) disinfection — chlorination is the main method in water works (also ozonation, UV irradiation); chlorine kills pathogenic bacteria and viruses (not cysts of Giardia/ova at usual doses), oxidises iron, manganese and hydrogen sulphide, destroys taste- and odour-producing compounds, controls algae and slime; principles: water should be clear (turbidity impedes), chlorine demand determined, contact period of at least 1 hour, free residual chlorine of 0.5 mg/L after 1 hour contact (minimum), breakpoint chlorination; methods — chlorine gas (chlorinating equipment — Paterson's chloronome), chloramine (combined chlorine — slower, longer-lasting residual, less taste), perchloron (high test hypochlorite — 60–70% available chlorine); superchlorination followed by dechlorination; tests for residual chlorine — orthotoluidine test (OT test — yellow colour; measures free and combined chlorine) and orthotoluidine–arsenite test (OTA test — distinguishes free and combined chlorine); DPD test. Purification of water on a small scale (household and individual): (a) boiling — satisfactory method of purifying water for household purposes; bring to a rolling boil for 1 minute (5–10 minutes at high altitude); kills all bacteria, spores, cysts, ova; removes temporary hardness (drives off CO2, precipitates calcium carbonate); taste altered (flat) — no residual protection, recontamination possible during storage (store in same vessel, use within 24 h); (b) chemical disinfection — (i) bleaching powder (chlorinated lime; CaOCl2; 33% available chlorine when fresh; unstable — deteriorates on exposure to air, light, moisture; used for disinfecting wells and small water supplies; household: 2.5 g bleaching powder per 1000 litres gives 0.7 ppm), (ii) chlorine solution (prepared from bleaching powder — 4 kg bleaching powder in 20 L water gives 5% chlorine solution; 6 drops per 20 L water), (iii) high test hypochlorite (HTH, perchloron; 60–70% available chlorine; stable), (iv) chlorine tablets (halazone — 4 mg/L; sodium dichloroisocyanurate tablets — one 0.5 g tablet disinfects 20 L water; NaDCC tablets supplied in emergencies/disasters), (v) iodine (2 drops of 2% ethanol solution of iodine per litre of water, contact 20–30 min; not for long-term use — thyroid), (vi) potassium permanganate (powerful oxidising agent but not satisfactory — destroys cholera vibrios but not other organisms, alters colour, smell, taste — no longer recommended), (vii) silver (colloidal), (viii) alum (sedimentation — clarification of turbid water); (c) filtration — household filters: ceramic candle filters (Pasteur–Chamberland — unglazed porcelain; Berkefeld — kieselguhr/infusorial earth; Katadyn — silver-coated; filter by size — bacteria retained but viruses pass; candles must be cleaned by scrubbing with brush under running water and boiled once a week), slow sand household filters (three-pot), biosand filters, activated charcoal; (d) other modern methods — reverse osmosis (RO — removes dissolved salts, fluoride, arsenic; wastage), ultraviolet irradiation (UV purifiers — kills bacteria, viruses; water must be clear; no residual), solar disinfection (SODIS — PET bottles exposed to sunlight 6 h), ozonation; (e) combined — sedimentation + filtration + chlorination ('flocculant-disinfectant' sachets). Disinfection of wells (most common source of rural water supply; open wells easily contaminated): best and cheapest method is bleaching powder; steps — (1) find the volume of water in the well: measure diameter (d) and depth of water column (h) in metres; volume = (3.14 × d² × h × 1000)/4 litres (1 m³ = 1000 L); (2) find the amount of bleaching powder required: estimate chlorine demand (Horrock's apparatus — 6 cups with graded doses of bleaching powder; first cup showing blue colour with starch-iodide indicator indicates dose); as a rule of thumb, 2.5 g of good quality bleaching powder (33% available chlorine) for 1000 litres of water to give 1 ppm (0.7 ppm) chlorine; (3) dissolve bleaching powder in a bucket of water to make a thin paste, add more water, stir, allow to settle, decant supernatant (chlorine solution) into the well using a rope and bucket; (4) contact period — allow 1 hour; (5) orthotoluidine test — perform after 1 hour; residual chlorine should be 0.5 mg/L; if not, repeat; best done at night when water is not drawn; wells disinfected once a week, and more frequently (daily/twice weekly) during epidemics; 'double pot' method (two concentric earthen pots with holes, filled with mixture of bleaching powder and coarse sand, lowered into well; provides continuous chlorination for 2–3 weeks for a well of ~4500 L with daily withdrawal of 360–450 L). Sanitary well — proper location (> 15 m from latrines/sources of contamination), lining, parapet wall, platform, drainage, covering, handpump, no common bucket.",
[["Slow sand vs rapid sand filter",[["Feature","Slow sand","Rapid sand"],["Type","Biological","Mechanical"],["Area","Large","Small"],["Rate of filtration","0.1–0.4 m³/m²/h","5–15 m³/m²/h"],["Effective sand size","0.2–0.3 mm","0.4–0.7 mm"],["Pretreatment","Plain sedimentation","Coagulation (alum), flocculation, sedimentation"],["Key layer","Vital layer (schmutzdecke)","Alum floc layer"],["Cleaning","Scraping top sand (weeks)","Backwashing (daily)"],["Bacterial removal","99.9%","98–99%"],["Operation","Simple, cheap","Skilled, costly"]]],
["Household purification",[["Method","Details"],["Boiling","Rolling boil 1 min; best; no residual"],["Bleaching powder","33% available Cl; 2.5 g/1000 L"],["Chlorine tablets","NaDCC 0.5 g for 20 L; halazone 4 mg/L"],["Iodine","2 drops 2% tincture/L, 20–30 min"],["Candle filters","Pasteur–Chamberland, Berkefeld; clean weekly"],["RO / UV / SODIS","Modern; RO removes salts, fluoride"]]],
["Well disinfection steps",["Volume = 3.14 × d² × h × 1000 / 4 (litres)","Chlorine demand (Horrock's apparatus) or 2.5 g BP per 1000 L","Make paste, decant supernatant into well","Contact 1 hour","OT test: residual 0.5 mg/L","Weekly; daily in epidemics; double pot method"]],
["Large-scale purification stages",[["Stage","Effect"],["Storage","Sedimentation, oxidation, 90% bacteria die (5–7 d)"],["Filtration","Removes 98–99.9% bacteria"],["Disinfection (chlorination)","Residual 0.5 mg/L after 1 h"]]]],
{i:"Safe drinking water prevents a large share of communicable diseases; purification methods differ by scale.",x:[["Water quality standards (BIS 10500)",["Coliforms/E. coli: nil in 100 mL","Turbidity < 1 NTU","pH 6.5–8.5","Fluoride 1.0 (1.5) mg/L","Residual chlorine ≥ 0.2 mg/L at tap"]]],f:"Raw water → storage (sedimentation) → [slow sand filter (vital layer) | coagulation → flocculation → sedimentation → rapid sand filter (backwash)] → chlorination (0.5 mg/L residual) → distribution\n Household: boil / chlorinate / filter / RO-UV",c:"Large-scale water purification combines storage, slow or rapid sand filtration and chlorination to achieve microbiologically safe water, while household methods such as boiling, chlorine tablets, bleaching powder and filters, and periodic well disinfection with bleaching powder, protect smaller supplies."});

Z("cm6",1,"cm",["Chlorination is the addition of chlorine to water to kill pathogens and leave a protective residual; breakpoint chlorination adds enough chlorine to satisfy all chlorine demand so that further chlorine appears as free residual; the orthotoluidine (OT) test measures residual chlorine (yellow colour).","Chlorine first 'eats up' impurities (chlorine demand) — only after the breakpoint does free chlorine remain to guard the water.",["Action: Cl2 + H2O → HOCl (hypochlorous acid — main disinfectant, 70–80× more effective) + HCl; HOCl ⇌ H⁺ + OCl⁻ (pH dependent; best at pH ~7); kills bacteria, viruses; oxidises iron, Mn, H2S; controls algae.","Principles: water clear (turbidity < 1 NTU), determine chlorine demand, contact period ≥ 1 hour, free residual 0.5 mg/L at the end of 1 hour (minimum), breakpoint chlorination; in epidemics superchlorination.","Breakpoint chlorination: chlorine demand = chlorine added − residual; initial chlorine reacts with reducing substances and ammonia (forming chloramines — combined chlorine); further addition destroys chloramines (residual falls) until breakpoint; beyond breakpoint free residual chlorine rises proportionally.","Orthotoluidine test (Ellms & Hauser): OT reagent added to water sample → yellow colour, compared with standards; measures free + combined chlorine; read within 10 s for free chlorine; OTA (orthotoluidine–arsenite) test distinguishes free from combined chlorine; DPD test alternative."],"Free residual chlorine 0.5 mg/L after 1 h contact; OT test → yellow."],
"Chlorination is the most widely used method of disinfection of water; it supplements, not replaces, filtration. Action of chlorine: when chlorine is added to water, it forms hypochlorous acid (HOCl) and hydrochloric acid: Cl2 + H2O → HOCl + HCl; hypochlorous acid then partially dissociates into hydrogen and hypochlorite ions: HOCl ⇌ H⁺ + OCl⁻; the disinfecting action is mainly due to HOCl (70–80 times more bactericidal than OCl⁻); the relative proportions depend on pH — at pH 7, ~80% HOCl; above pH 8.5 mostly OCl⁻ (less effective), so chlorination is more efficient at slightly acidic to neutral pH; chlorine also combines with ammonia and nitrogenous compounds to form chloramines (combined available chlorine — monochloramine, dichloramine, nitrogen trichloride) which are weaker but more persistent disinfectants; free available chlorine = HOCl + OCl⁻. Effects of chlorine: kills pathogenic bacteria (vegetative forms) and viruses (poliovirus and hepatitis A require higher doses and longer contact), but has no effect on spores, Giardia and Entamoeba cysts, Cryptosporidium oocysts and helminth ova (at usual doses) — these are removed by filtration; oxidises iron, manganese and hydrogen sulphide; destroys some taste- and odour-producing constituents; controls algae and slime organisms; aids coagulation. Principles of chlorination: (1) the water to be chlorinated should be clear and free from turbidity (turbidity impedes efficient chlorination — organisms protected inside particles); (2) the chlorine demand of the water should be estimated — chlorine demand is the amount of chlorine needed to destroy bacteria and to oxidise all organic matter and ammoniacal substances present in the water; the point at which the chlorine demand of the water is met is called the breakpoint; if further chlorine is added beyond the breakpoint, free chlorine (HOCl and OCl⁻) begins to appear in the water; (3) the presence of free residual chlorine for a contact period of at least one hour is essential to kill bacteria and viruses; (4) the minimum recommended concentration of free chlorine is 0.5 mg/L for one hour (WHO); the free residual chlorine provides a margin of safety against subsequent microbial contamination during storage and distribution (at consumer end ≥ 0.2 mg/L); (5) the sum of chlorine demand of the specific water plus the free residual chlorine of 0.5 mg/L constitutes the correct dose of chlorine to be applied. Breakpoint chlorination: when increasing doses of chlorine are added to water containing ammonia/organic matter and the residual chlorine is measured after contact time, a characteristic curve is obtained — (a) initially chlorine is consumed by reducing substances (Fe²⁺, Mn²⁺, H2S, nitrites, organic matter) — no residual; (b) further chlorine reacts with ammonia forming chloramines (combined residual chlorine rises); (c) with more chlorine, chloramines are oxidised and destroyed (to nitrogen gas, nitrous oxide, nitrogen trichloride) — combined residual falls (chlorine is consumed); (d) the lowest point of this dip is the breakpoint — at this point all ammonia and chloramines are destroyed; (e) beyond the breakpoint any added chlorine appears as free residual chlorine, which rises linearly with dose; breakpoint chlorination ensures free residual chlorine (stronger disinfectant), removal of taste and odour (chloramines and organic compounds destroyed), and is the method of choice in water works; chlorine demand = chlorine added − chlorine residual (at a contact period of 1 h); 'marginal chlorination' (dose to give residual just beyond breakpoint — for clear water) vs 'superchlorination' (much higher doses — 5–10 mg/L — during epidemics or for heavily polluted water, followed by dechlorination with sodium thiosulphate or sulphur dioxide). Methods of chlorination (large scale): (1) chlorine gas — cheapest, quickest, easiest; requires chlorinating equipment (chlorinators, e.g., Paterson's chloronome) for measuring and regulating the flow; toxic and corrosive — skilled handling; (2) chloramine — combination of chlorine and ammonia; slower action, does not give taste, more persistent residual; used in distribution systems; (3) perchloron (high test hypochlorite, HTH — calcium hypochlorite 60–70% available chlorine); for small installations; (4) bleaching powder (33% available chlorine) — for wells and small supplies. Tests for residual chlorine: (a) orthotoluidine (OT) test (Ellms and Hauser, 1913) — enables both free and combined chlorine in water to be determined with speed and accuracy; reagent — orthotoluidine dihydrochloride dissolved in hydrochloric acid (10% HCl); procedure — 0.1 mL of OT reagent added to 1 mL of water sample (or 10 drops to 10 mL) in a comparator tube; mixed; in presence of chlorine a yellow colour develops — the intensity of colour is proportional to the amount of chlorine present; compared against standard coloured discs/glass standards (Lovibond comparator) or chart; colour read within 10 seconds represents free chlorine; colour read after 15–20 minutes (maximum colour) represents total (free + combined) chlorine; the difference gives combined chlorine; interference by nitrites, iron, manganese (false positive); orthotoluidine is a suspected carcinogen; (b) orthotoluidine–arsenite (OTA) test — modification to determine free and combined chlorine separately and to eliminate interference from nitrites, iron and manganese; sodium arsenite (dechlorinating agent) is used; three tubes (A, B, C) with sequential addition of OT and arsenite at timed intervals; results: free chlorine = A − B... (calculations as per protocol); (c) DPD test (N,N-diethyl-p-phenylenediamine — pink/red colour; now widely used, safer); (d) chlorine test kits/strips used by field workers and ASHAs (for ensuring safe water in villages). Advantages of chlorination: effective, cheap, easy to apply and measure, provides residual protection; disadvantages: formation of disinfection by-products (trihalomethanes — potential carcinogens) when organic matter is present, taste and odour complaints, ineffective against cysts, oocysts, ova at normal doses, toxic gas handling.",
[["Principles of chlorination",["Water clear (turbidity < 1 NTU)","Determine chlorine demand","Contact period ≥ 1 hour","Free residual chlorine 0.5 mg/L after 1 h","Dose = chlorine demand + 0.5 mg/L residual","Breakpoint chlorination preferred"]],
["Breakpoint curve phases",[["Phase","Event"],["1","Chlorine consumed by reducing agents (no residual)"],["2","Chloramines formed (combined residual ↑)"],["3","Chloramines destroyed (residual ↓)"],["Breakpoint","All demand satisfied; lowest residual"],["4","Free residual chlorine ↑ linearly"]]],
["Tests for residual chlorine",[["Test","Principle","Measures"],["Orthotoluidine (OT)","Yellow colour with chlorine","Free (10 s) + total (15–20 min)"],["Orthotoluidine–arsenite (OTA)","Arsenite dechlorination","Free vs combined; removes interference"],["DPD","Pink/red colour","Free and total; safer"]]]],
{f:"Chlorine dose ↑ → [reducing substances consumed] → [chloramines formed: combined residual ↑] → [chloramines destroyed: residual ↓] → BREAKPOINT → free residual ↑\n Target: free residual 0.5 mg/L after 1 h (OT test yellow)",c:"Chlorination disinfects water through hypochlorous acid; breakpoint chlorination satisfies all chlorine demand so that protective free residual chlorine (0.5 mg/L after one hour) remains, verified by the orthotoluidine or OTA test."});

Z("cm6",2,"cm",["Air pollution is the presence in air of substances (particulate matter, SO2, NOx, CO, ozone, lead) at concentrations harmful to health and environment; India has some of the world's most polluted cities, and air pollution causes ~16–17 lakh deaths annually through respiratory and cardiovascular disease; control relies on source reduction, standards, monitoring (AQI) and the National Clean Air Programme.","Air pollution is an invisible epidemic — tiny particles (PM2.5) go deep into the lungs and bloodstream, causing heart attacks, strokes and lung disease.",["Sources: outdoor — vehicles, industries, thermal power plants, construction dust, crop-residue burning, waste burning; indoor — solid biomass fuels (chulhas), tobacco smoke, kerosene, mosquito coils.","Pollutants: PM10, PM2.5 (most harmful), SO2, NO2, CO (carboxyhaemoglobin), ozone (photochemical smog), lead, benzene; NAAQS India — PM2.5 annual 40 µg/m³ (WHO 2021: 5), PM10 60 (WHO 15).","Health effects: acute — respiratory irritation, asthma exacerbation, COPD exacerbation, ARI in children, MI; chronic — COPD, lung cancer, IHD, stroke, LBW, cognitive effects; London smog 1952, Bhopal 1984 (MIC).","Control: containment, replacement (cleaner fuels — CNG, LPG — PM Ujjwala Yojana, BS-VI vehicles, EVs), dilution (green belts, tall chimneys), legislation (Air Act 1981, EPA 1986), monitoring (NAMP, AQI — 6 categories), National Clean Air Programme (2019 — 20–30%→40% PM reduction in 131 cities by 2026), Graded Response Action Plan (Delhi-NCR), masks, health advisories."],"Air pollution control 'C-R-D-L': Containment, Replacement, Dilution, Legislation."],
"Air pollution is the presence in the ambient atmosphere of substances, generally resulting from human activity, in sufficient concentration, present for a sufficient time and under circumstances that interfere significantly with comfort, health or welfare of persons or with full use or enjoyment of property (WHO). Burden: WHO estimates 99% of the world's population breathes air exceeding WHO guideline limits; ambient and household air pollution together cause ~67 lakh premature deaths annually; India — 14 of the world's 20 most polluted cities (IQAir); Lancet Planetary Health/ICMR (2019) — 16.7 lakh deaths (17.8% of total) attributable to air pollution (ambient PM 9.8 lakh, household 6.1 lakh); air pollution is the second leading risk factor for disease burden in India; economic loss ~1.4% of GDP. Sources: (1) outdoor (ambient) — automobiles/vehicular exhaust (CO, NOx, hydrocarbons, PM, lead — now removed from petrol), industries (thermal power plants — SO2, fly ash; refineries, cement, brick kilns, smelters), domestic sources (burning of coal, wood, biomass), construction and road dust, agricultural crop-residue (stubble) burning (Punjab–Haryana — Delhi winter smog), open burning of municipal waste, firecrackers, natural (dust storms, volcanoes, forest fires, pollen); (2) indoor (household) — cooking and heating with solid fuels (wood, dung, crop residues, coal) in traditional chulhas with poor ventilation (~40% Indian households still use them partly) — PM, CO, PAHs, formaldehyde; tobacco smoke (second-hand), kerosene lamps, mosquito coils, incense, building materials (asbestos, formaldehyde, radon), biological (moulds, dust mites). Major pollutants: particulate matter — PM10 (coarse, < 10 µm; deposited in upper airways) and PM2.5 (fine, < 2.5 µm; reach alveoli and bloodstream — most damaging), ultrafine particles; sulphur dioxide (from burning of sulphur-containing fuels — coal; respiratory irritant, acid rain), oxides of nitrogen (NO2 — vehicles; respiratory irritant, precursor of ozone and smog), carbon monoxide (incomplete combustion; binds haemoglobin with 200–250 times affinity of oxygen — carboxyhaemoglobin; headache, cognitive impairment, death), ozone (secondary pollutant — photochemical reaction of NOx and VOCs in sunlight; photochemical smog — Los Angeles type; eye and lung irritation), lead (formerly from petrol; neurotoxic in children), hydrocarbons/volatile organic compounds (benzene — leukaemia), polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons (benzo(a)pyrene — carcinogen), ammonia, heavy metals. Indicators/monitoring: National Ambient Air Quality Standards (NAAQS 2009, CPCB) — 12 pollutants; e.g., PM2.5: annual 40 µg/m³, 24-h 60; PM10: annual 60, 24-h 100; SO2: annual 50, 24-h 80; NO2: annual 40, 24-h 80; WHO Air Quality Guidelines 2021 (stricter) — PM2.5 annual 5 µg/m³, 24-h 15; PM10 annual 15, 24-h 45; National Air Quality Monitoring Programme (NAMP — > 1500 manual and continuous stations); National Air Quality Index (AQI, launched 2014) — based on eight pollutants (PM10, PM2.5, NO2, SO2, CO, O3, NH3, Pb); six categories with colour codes and health advisories: good (0–50), satisfactory (51–100), moderately polluted (101–200), poor (201–300), very poor (301–400), severe (401–500); smoke and SO2 index, coefficient of haze. Meteorological factors: temperature inversion (warm air layer traps cool polluted air near ground — winter, valleys), wind speed and direction, humidity, topography. Health effects: acute — irritation of eyes, nose and throat, cough, wheezing, exacerbation of asthma and COPD, acute respiratory infections in children (pneumonia — leading cause of under-5 deaths), cardiovascular events (MI, arrhythmias, stroke), increased hospital admissions and deaths during episodes (London smog December 1952 — ~4000–12,000 excess deaths from SO2 and smoke under inversion; Meuse Valley 1930; Donora 1948; Bhopal gas tragedy 1984 — methyl isocyanate); chronic — COPD, reduced lung function and growth in children, asthma development, lung cancer (outdoor air pollution and PM classified as Group 1 carcinogen by IARC 2013), ischaemic heart disease, stroke, hypertension, diabetes, adverse pregnancy outcomes (low birth weight, preterm birth, stillbirth), cognitive impairment, dementia, cataract (indoor smoke), tuberculosis (indoor smoke); effects on environment — acid rain, damage to vegetation and buildings (Taj Mahal yellowing — 'stone cancer'), reduced visibility, climate change (black carbon, ozone), ozone layer depletion (CFCs). Prevention and control (WHO principles): (1) containment — prevention of escape of toxic substances into the ambient air: engineering methods — enclosure, ventilation, air cleaning (filters, electrostatic precipitators, scrubbers, cyclone separators, catalytic converters in vehicles); (2) replacement — replacing technological processes causing pollution by new processes that do not: use of cleaner fuels — CNG for public transport (Delhi — Supreme Court 1998), LPG for cooking (Pradhan Mantri Ujjwala Yojana 2016 — free LPG connections to > 10 crore BPL households), electric vehicles (FAME scheme), Bharat Stage VI emission norms (leapfrogged from BS-IV in 2020), unleaded petrol (2000), flue gas desulphurisation in thermal plants, improved cookstoves, solar energy; (3) dilution — dilution within the self-cleansing capacity of the environment: establishment of green belts between industrial and residential areas, tall chimneys, urban forestry, zoning; (4) legislation — Air (Prevention and Control of Pollution) Act 1981 (CPCB and SPCBs), Environment (Protection) Act 1986, Motor Vehicles Act (pollution under control certificates), National Green Tribunal (2010), Commission for Air Quality Management in NCR (2021), ban on stubble burning, firecracker restrictions, Graded Response Action Plan (GRAP — stage-wise measures in Delhi-NCR based on AQI — stopping construction, odd–even, closing schools), Construction and Demolition Waste Rules; (5) international action — WHO, UNEP, Paris Agreement, Montreal Protocol; (6) National Clean Air Programme (NCAP, January 2019) — time-bound national strategy for 131 non-attainment cities: target 20–30% reduction in PM10/PM2.5 concentrations by 2024 from 2017 baseline, revised to up to 40% reduction by 2025–26; city-specific clean air action plans, expansion of monitoring network, source apportionment studies, public awareness, PRANA portal; (7) National Programme on Climate Change and Human Health (NPCCHH) — health sector response, air pollution and health surveillance, sentinel sites for ARI; (8) personal protection — N95 masks during high pollution, avoiding outdoor exercise on poor AQI days, indoor air purifiers, ventilation; health advisories for vulnerable groups (children, elderly, those with heart and lung disease, pregnant women); (9) health education and community participation, tobacco control.",
[["Major pollutants and effects",[["Pollutant","Main source","Health effect"],["PM2.5 / PM10","Vehicles, industry, biomass, dust","COPD, IHD, stroke, lung cancer, ARI"],["SO2","Coal burning","Bronchospasm, acid rain"],["NO2","Vehicles","Airway irritation, ozone precursor"],["CO","Incomplete combustion","Carboxyhaemoglobin, hypoxia"],["Ozone","Photochemical (NOx + VOC)","Lung and eye irritation"],["Lead","Formerly petrol, industry","Neurotoxicity in children"],["Benzene, PAH","Vehicles, smoke","Cancer"]]],
["AQI categories (India)",[["AQI","Category"],["0–50","Good"],["51–100","Satisfactory"],["101–200","Moderately polluted"],["201–300","Poor"],["301–400","Very poor"],["401–500","Severe"]]],
["Control measures",[["Principle","Examples"],["Containment","Filters, scrubbers, electrostatic precipitators, catalytic converters"],["Replacement","CNG, LPG (Ujjwala), BS-VI, EVs, cleaner technology"],["Dilution","Green belts, tall chimneys, zoning"],["Legislation","Air Act 1981, EPA 1986, NGT, CAQM, GRAP"],["Programmes","NCAP 2019 (131 cities, 40% PM reduction by 2026), NPCCHH"],["Personal","N95 masks, advisories, avoid outdoor exertion"]]]],
{c:"Air pollution from vehicles, industry, biomass and crop burning is a leading health risk in India; control combines containment, replacement with cleaner fuels and technology, dilution, legislation, AQI-based monitoring and the National Clean Air Programme."});

Z("cm6",3,"cm",["Biomedical waste (BMW) is waste generated during diagnosis, treatment, immunisation or research in humans/animals; under the Bio-Medical Waste Management Rules 2016 (amended 2018, 2019), it is segregated at source into four colour-coded categories — yellow, red, white (translucent) and blue — and treated by incineration, autoclaving, shredding, chemical disinfection or deep burial.","Segregation at source is the key — put each type of waste in the right coloured bag so that infectious waste is burnt or sterilised and recyclables are disinfected and recycled.",["Hazards: 10–25% of hospital waste is hazardous; risks — needle-stick injuries (HIV, HBV, HCV), infections, chemical/radiation exposure, environmental pollution, illegal reuse of syringes.","Yellow: human/animal anatomical waste, soiled waste (dressings, cotton), expired/discarded medicines, chemical waste, microbiology/lab waste (pre-treated), chemical liquid — incineration/plasma pyrolysis/deep burial.","Red: contaminated recyclable plastic waste — tubing, bottles, IV sets, catheters, urine bags, syringes without needles, gloves — autoclaving/microwaving/hydroclaving + shredding → recycling (not incinerated).","White (translucent, puncture-proof): sharps — needles, syringes with fixed needles, scalpels, blades — autoclave/dry heat sterilisation + shredding/encapsulation; Blue (cardboard box with blue marking): glassware, metallic body implants — disinfection/autoclaving → recycling; storage ≤ 48 h; bar-coding; Common Bio-medical Waste Treatment Facility (CBWTF); pre-treatment of lab waste on-site; phase-out of chlorinated plastic bags; training, immunisation of handlers (hepatitis B), records, annual report."],"BMW 2016: Yellow (incinerate), Red (autoclave + recycle plastics), White (sharps), Blue (glass/metal)."],
"Biomedical waste means any waste which is generated during the diagnosis, treatment or immunisation of human beings or animals or research activities pertaining thereto or in the production or testing of biologicals or in health camps (BMW Management Rules 2016). Quantity and composition: Indian hospitals generate ~1–2 kg/bed/day; ~85% of health care waste is general, non-hazardous (comparable to domestic waste), ~10% infectious and ~5% non-infectious hazardous (chemical, pharmaceutical, radioactive); India generates ~700–800 tonnes/day (higher during COVID-19). Hazards: (1) infection risk — needle-stick and sharps injuries to health workers, waste handlers and rag-pickers (risk of HBV 30%, HCV 3%, HIV 0.3% per needle-stick from infected source), nosocomial infections, spread of drug-resistant organisms; (2) reuse of disposable syringes and needles (unsafe injections) — hepatitis and HIV transmission; (3) chemical hazards (disinfectants, mercury, cytotoxic drugs), (4) radiation hazards, (5) environmental pollution — air pollution (dioxins and furans from improper incineration of chlorinated plastics), soil and water contamination, (6) injuries and aesthetics, (7) public health risk to scavengers and community. Legal framework: Environment (Protection) Act 1986 → Bio-Medical Waste (Management and Handling) Rules 1998 (10 categories, 4 colours) → Bio-Medical Waste Management Rules 2016 (notified by MoEFCC on 28 March 2016; amended 2018 and 2019) — salient features: scope expanded to vaccination camps, blood donation camps, surgical camps, other health care activities; categories reduced to four colour codes; pre-treatment of laboratory, microbiological waste and blood bags on-site by disinfection or sterilisation as per WHO/NACO guidelines before handing over to CBWTF; phase-out of use of chlorinated plastic bags (excluding blood bags) and gloves within 2 years (by 27 March 2019); bar-code system for bags/containers containing BMW; stringent emission standards for incinerators (dioxins, furans; residence time 2 s in secondary chamber at 1050 ± 50 °C); no occupier shall establish on-site treatment facility if a CBWTF is available within 75 km; training of all health care workers and immunisation (hepatitis B, tetanus) of all workers handling BMW; maintain records, report major accidents, submit annual report to State Pollution Control Board, display on website; liquid waste segregation and pre-treatment; storage of segregated BMW not beyond 48 hours; responsibility of occupier and operator defined; deep burial permitted only in rural/remote areas without CBWTF access. Steps in BMW management: (1) survey of waste generated; (2) segregation at source — the most important step (at point of generation into colour-coded bags/containers as per category; reduces volume of hazardous waste, prevents mixing with general waste); (3) collection and labelling (biohazard/cytotoxic symbol, bar code); (4) interim storage (≤ 48 h); (5) transportation (dedicated covered trolleys within facility; closed vehicles to CBWTF); (6) treatment and disposal; (7) record keeping, monitoring, training and occupational safety. Categories, segregation, treatment and disposal (Schedule I, BMW Rules 2016 as amended): Yellow category (non-chlorinated yellow plastic bags / containers): (a) human anatomical waste — tissues, organs, body parts, fetus < viability period; (b) animal anatomical waste — carcasses, body parts, organs from veterinary hospitals and research; (c) soiled waste — items contaminated with blood/body fluids such as dressings, plaster casts, cotton swabs, bags containing residual or discarded blood and blood components; (d) expired or discarded medicines — antibiotics, cytotoxic drugs (cytotoxic labelled) including all items contaminated with cytotoxic drugs, along with glass or plastic ampoules, vials etc.; (e) chemical waste — chemicals used in production of biologicals, used or discarded disinfectants; (f) chemical liquid waste — liquid waste generated from laboratories, washing, cleaning, housekeeping, disinfecting (separate collection system leading to effluent treatment); (g) discarded linen, mattresses, beddings contaminated with blood or body fluids; (h) microbiology, biotechnology and other clinical laboratory waste — blood bags, laboratory cultures, stocks or specimens of microorganisms, live or attenuated vaccines, human and animal cell cultures, residual toxins, dishes and devices used for cultures (pre-treated on-site by autoclaving/microwaving/hydroclaving before sending for incineration); treatment — incineration, plasma pyrolysis or deep burial (only in rural areas, for anatomical and soiled waste); expired medicines returned to manufacturer or incinerated at > 1200 °C; chemical waste — disposal at hazardous waste facility; liquid chemical waste — neutralisation and effluent treatment; linen — non-chlorinated chemical disinfection followed by incineration/plasma pyrolysis. Red category (non-chlorinated red plastic bags or containers): contaminated waste (recyclable) — wastes generated from disposable items such as tubing, bottles, intravenous tubes and sets, catheters, urine bags, syringes (without needles and fixed needle syringes with cut needles), vacutainers with their needles cut, and gloves; treatment — autoclaving or microwaving/hydroclaving followed by shredding or mutilation (or combination of sterilisation and shredding); treated waste sent to registered/authorised recyclers or for energy recovery or plastics to diesel/fuel oil or road making; not to be sent to landfill; not incinerated (chlorinated plastics produce dioxins). White category (translucent, puncture-proof, leak-proof, tamper-proof containers): waste sharps including metals — needles, syringes with fixed needles, needles from needle tip cutter or burner, scalpels, blades, or any other contaminated sharp object that may cause puncture and cuts, including both used, discarded and contaminated metal sharps; treatment — autoclaving or dry heat sterilisation followed by shredding or mutilation or encapsulation in metal container or cement concrete; sent for final disposal to iron foundries (with consent) or sanitary landfill or designated concrete waste sharp pit; use of needle cutters/destroyers at point of use (hub cutters) encouraged. Blue category (puncture-proof, leak-proof cardboard boxes or containers with blue coloured marking): (a) glassware — broken or discarded and contaminated glass including medicine vials and ampoules except those contaminated with cytotoxic wastes; (b) metallic body implants; treatment — disinfection (by soaking the washed glass waste after cleaning with detergent and sodium hypochlorite treatment) or through autoclaving or microwaving or hydroclaving, then sent for recycling. Treatment technologies: incineration (double-chamber; primary chamber 800 ± 50 °C, secondary chamber 1050 ± 50 °C with 2 s residence; for anatomical, soiled, pharmaceutical waste; ash to secured landfill; risk of dioxins), plasma pyrolysis/gasification, autoclaving (steam sterilisation at 121 °C, 15 psi, 60 min or 135 °C, 31 psi, 45 min — gravity/vacuum type), microwaving, hydroclaving, chemical disinfection (1% sodium hypochlorite, at least 30 min contact), shredding, encapsulation, deep burial (pit 2 m deep, half filled with waste, covered with lime, then soil; away from habitation and water sources — rural areas only), secured landfill. Liquid waste: pre-treatment and disinfection before discharge into effluent treatment plant/sewer meeting standards. Common Bio-medical Waste Treatment and Disposal Facility (CBWTF): centralised facility serving health care facilities in a 75 km radius — collection, transport (GPS tracking), treatment (incinerator, autoclave, shredder), disposal; ~200 CBWTFs in India. Responsibilities: occupier (health care facility) — segregation, pre-treatment, safe storage, handover to CBWTF, training, immunisation, records, BMW committee (in hospitals with ≥ 30 beds), annual report by 30 June; State Pollution Control Board — authorisation and monitoring; CPCB — guidelines.",
[["Colour coding (BMW Rules 2016)",[["Category","Waste","Treatment / disposal"],["Yellow","Anatomical, soiled waste, expired medicines, chemical waste, lab/micro waste (pre-treated), contaminated linen","Incineration, plasma pyrolysis, deep burial (rural)"],["Red","Contaminated recyclable plastics — tubing, IV sets, catheters, urine bags, syringes without needles, gloves","Autoclave/microwave/hydroclave + shredding → recycling"],["White (translucent)","Sharps — needles, fixed-needle syringes, blades, scalpels","Autoclave/dry heat + shredding/encapsulation → foundry/landfill/sharp pit"],["Blue (cardboard box)","Glassware (vials, ampoules), metallic implants","Disinfection/autoclave → recycling"]]],
["Salient features of 2016 Rules",["Four colour categories (from 10)","Pre-treatment of lab, micro waste and blood bags on-site","Phase-out of chlorinated plastic bags and gloves","Bar-coding of bags and containers","Storage ≤ 48 hours","Stringent incinerator emission standards (dioxins)","CBWTF within 75 km — no on-site incinerator","Training and hepatitis B immunisation of workers","Annual report to SPCB"]],
["Steps of BMW management",["Survey","Segregation at source (most important)","Collection and labelling (bar code)","Interim storage (≤ 48 h)","Transport","Treatment","Disposal","Records, training, monitoring"]]],
{i:"Proper biomedical waste management protects health workers, patients, the community and the environment; segregation at source is its foundation.",x:[["Treatment technologies",["Incineration (800/1050 °C, 2 s)","Autoclaving (121 °C, 15 psi, 60 min)","Microwaving, hydroclaving","Chemical disinfection (1% hypochlorite)","Shredding, encapsulation","Deep burial (rural)"]]],f:"Point of generation → segregation (Yellow / Red / White / Blue) → bar-coded collection → storage ≤ 48 h → transport → CBWTF → incineration / autoclave + shred / sterilise + recycle → disposal",c:"Under the BMW Management Rules 2016, biomedical waste is segregated at source into yellow, red, white and blue categories with category-specific treatment — incineration, autoclaving and shredding, sharps sterilisation and glass disinfection — through common treatment facilities, with training, immunisation and record keeping."});

Z("cm6",4,"cm",["Occupational diseases arise from exposures at work; pneumoconioses are lung diseases caused by inhalation of mineral dusts (silicosis — silica, coal workers' pneumoconiosis, asbestosis, byssinosis — cotton, bagassosis, farmer's lung), and lead poisoning (plumbism) occurs in battery, smelting, paint and printing industries; prevention relies on engineering controls, medical surveillance and legislation.","Occupational diseases are entirely man-made and therefore entirely preventable — control the dust or fumes at the source before they reach the worker's lungs.",["Pneumoconiosis: particles 0.5–3 µm reach alveoli; silicosis (free silica — mining, quarrying, sandblasting, stone cutting, glass, pottery; most common & serious in India; 'snowstorm' X-ray, eggshell calcification of hilar nodes, ↑ TB risk; notifiable), anthracosis (coal), asbestosis (asbestos — mesothelioma, lung cancer; banned mining), byssinosis (cotton dust — 'Monday fever', chest tightness), bagassosis (sugarcane — hypersensitivity), farmer's lung (mouldy hay — thermophilic actinomycetes).","Lead poisoning: sources — battery manufacture/recycling, smelting, paints, printing, glazing, plumbing, petrol (historical); routes — inhalation (main), ingestion; features — abdominal colic, constipation, Burtonian blue line on gums, anaemia (basophilic stippling), wrist/foot drop, encephalopathy (children), nephropathy, hypertension, infertility.","Lead diagnosis/limits: blood lead (> 40 µg/dL workers action; children ≥ 5 µg/dL), urinary coproporphyrin, ALA in urine, ZPP; treatment — remove from exposure, chelation (CaNa2EDTA, DMSA, BAL).","Prevention: medical (pre-placement and periodic examinations, chest X-ray ILO classification, spirometry, blood lead), engineering (substitution, wet methods, enclosure, local exhaust ventilation, dust suppression), personal protection (respirators), legislation (Factories Act 1948, Mines Act 1952, OSH Code 2020), education, compensation (ESI, Employees' Compensation Act)."],"Pneumoconiosis prevention: substitution, wet drilling, exhaust ventilation, respirators, periodic X-ray."],
"Occupational health deals with all aspects of health and safety in the workplace — promotion and maintenance of the highest degree of physical, mental and social well-being of workers in all occupations (ILO/WHO). Occupational hazards: physical (heat, cold, light, noise, vibration, radiation, pressure), chemical (dusts, gases, metals and their compounds, solvents), biological (anthrax, brucellosis, leptospirosis, tetanus, TB in health workers), mechanical (injuries from machinery), psychosocial (stress, shift work), ergonomic. Pneumoconiosis: lung disease due to inhalation and retention of dust particles in the lungs, and the tissue reaction to their presence (ILO); dust particles of size 0.5–3 µm (respirable) reach and deposit in alveoli; larger particles are trapped in upper airways and removed; reaction depends on chemical composition, size, concentration and duration of exposure, individual susceptibility. Types: (1) inorganic (mineral) dusts — silicosis (free crystalline silica — quartz, flint), anthracosis/coal workers' pneumoconiosis (coal dust), asbestosis (asbestos fibres), siderosis (iron oxide — welders; benign), berylliosis, talcosis, kaolinosis, stannosis; (2) organic dusts (hypersensitivity pneumonitis/extrinsic allergic alveolitis) — byssinosis (cotton, flax, hemp dust — textile mills), bagassosis (bagasse — sugarcane fibre; thermoactinomyces sacchari), farmer's lung (mouldy hay — Saccharopolyspora rectivirgula/thermophilic actinomycetes), tobacco-workers' lung, mushroom-workers' lung, bird-fancier's lung. Silicosis: the most important and serious occupational disease in India (estimated > 1 crore workers exposed; prevalence among stone workers 20–50%); causative agent — inhalation of free crystalline silica (SiO2) dust; occupations — mining (gold, coal, mica, lead, zinc, copper, iron), quarrying, stone crushing and cutting, sandblasting, slate pencil industry (Mandsaur), agate industry (Khambhat, Gujarat), foundries, glass and pottery, ceramics, cement, construction, tunnelling, artificial stone (engineered quartz) countertop fabrication; pathogenesis — silica particles ingested by alveolar macrophages cause their death, releasing silica and fibrogenic cytokines (TNF, IL-1, TGF-β), leading to formation of silicotic nodules (whorled hyalinised collagen) and progressive fibrosis; incubation (latent period) 10–20 years in chronic silicosis (accelerated 5–10 years; acute silicosis within months of massive exposure — silicoproteinosis); clinical — dry cough, progressive dyspnoea, chest pain; complications — pulmonary tuberculosis (silicotuberculosis — risk increased 3–30 times; silica impairs macrophage function), progressive massive fibrosis, cor pulmonale, lung cancer (silica — Group 1 carcinogen), autoimmune diseases, chronic kidney disease; X-ray — small rounded opacities in upper zones ('snowstorm' appearance), eggshell calcification of hilar lymph nodes, conglomerate masses in PMF; ILO classification of radiographs; no specific treatment — irreversible; management — removal from exposure, treatment of TB (and TB preventive therapy), symptomatic, oxygen, lung transplant; notifiable disease under Factories Act and Mines Act; compensation; Rajasthan Pneumoconiosis Policy 2019 (financial assistance, rehabilitation). Asbestosis: asbestos (fibrous silicates — chrysotile/white, amphiboles — crocidolite/blue most dangerous, amosite); occupations — asbestos mining and milling, asbestos-cement sheets and pipes, brake linings, insulation, shipbreaking (Alang); latent period 10–20 years for asbestosis, 20–40 years for mesothelioma; lower lobe interstitial fibrosis, pleural plaques, asbestos bodies (ferruginous bodies) in sputum; risk of bronchogenic carcinoma (synergistic with smoking — 50–90 times), malignant mesothelioma of pleura/peritoneum, laryngeal and ovarian cancer; India banned asbestos mining (1986) but allows import and use of chrysotile products. Byssinosis: textile mill workers (cotton carding, spinning); 'Monday fever/chest tightness' — symptoms (chest tightness, cough, dyspnoea) on first day back at work after weekend, improving during the week; progresses to chronic bronchitis and COPD; prevention — dust control, steaming cotton. Lead poisoning (plumbism): lead is the most widely used heavy metal and an important occupational and environmental toxicant; occupational sources — manufacture and recycling of lead-acid storage batteries (most important in India — informal recycling units), lead smelting and refining, ship-breaking, manufacture of lead pigments and paints (red lead, white lead), pottery glazing, printing (type foundries), plumbing, soldering, cable making, rubber industry, glass, ammunition, painters scraping old paint; non-occupational — leaded petrol (banned in India in 2000), lead paints in toys and houses, contaminated traditional medicines (ayurvedic bhasmas), cosmetics (sindoor, kohl/surma), contaminated spices (turmeric adulteration), water from lead pipes, contaminated soil, food cans; children particularly vulnerable (higher absorption, pica, developing nervous system); routes of absorption — inhalation of fumes and dust (most important occupationally), ingestion (most important in children — hand-to-mouth), skin (organic lead — tetraethyl lead); distribution — blood (99% in RBCs), soft tissues, bones (~90% of body burden; long half-life — decades; mobilised in pregnancy, osteoporosis); excretion — urine and faeces; mechanism — binds sulfhydryl groups, inhibits enzymes of haem synthesis (δ-aminolaevulinic acid dehydratase and ferrochelatase — ALA and protoporphyrin accumulate; zinc protoporphyrin), interferes with calcium-dependent processes (neurotransmission), causes oxidative stress; clinical features — (a) abdominal: anorexia, metallic taste, abdominal colic ('lead colic'), constipation (or diarrhoea), (b) Burtonian blue line (lead sulphide deposit on gum margin — sign of exposure), (c) haematological: anaemia (microcytic hypochromic or normocytic; reduced haem synthesis, haemolysis), basophilic stippling of red cells (punctate basophilia), reticulocytosis, (d) neuromuscular: peripheral motor neuropathy — wrist drop and foot drop (radial and peroneal nerves), fatigue, myalgia, (e) CNS: lead encephalopathy (more in children — headache, irritability, convulsions, coma; raised ICP), cognitive deficits, reduced IQ (no safe level in children), behavioural problems, (f) renal: chronic interstitial nephropathy, Fanconi syndrome, saturnine gout, hypertension, (g) reproductive: infertility, abortions, stillbirths, low birth weight, (h) others — lead lines on X-ray of long bones in children (metaphyseal bands), skin pallor ('lead pallor'); diagnosis — blood lead level (most reliable indicator of recent exposure; normal adults < 10 µg/dL; WHO/CDC: children reference level ≥ 3.5–5 µg/dL — 'no safe level'; workers — action at > 40 µg/dL, removal from exposure at > 60 µg/dL or > 50 on repeat (OSHA)), zinc protoporphyrin/free erythrocyte protoporphyrin (raised), urinary δ-ALA (> 5 mg/L), urinary coproporphyrin (> 150 µg/L — screening test), urinary lead (> 0.08 mg/L — absorption), basophilic stippling, X-ray fluorescence of bone (body burden), CaNa2EDTA mobilisation test; management — removal from exposure (most important), supportive care, chelation therapy in symptomatic cases or high blood lead — CaNa2EDTA IV, BAL (with EDTA in encephalopathy), succimer (DMSA — oral; children with BLL 45–69 µg/dL), D-penicillamine; nutritional support (iron, calcium, zinc reduce lead absorption). Prevention of occupational diseases (pneumoconiosis and lead poisoning): (1) medical measures — pre-placement (pre-employment) examination (baseline chest X-ray, lung function, blood lead; exclude susceptible — TB, respiratory disease, anaemia, kidney disease, pregnancy for lead), periodic medical examinations (every 6–12 months: clinical, chest X-ray (ILO classification), spirometry; blood lead, ZPP, urinary ALA/coproporphyrin for lead workers), early detection and removal from exposure, treatment and rehabilitation, TB screening for silica workers, health education, immunisation, notification of occupational diseases; (2) engineering measures — substitution (using less hazardous material — non-silica abrasives, lead-free paints and solders), design of building, good housekeeping, enclosure and isolation of dusty processes, local exhaust ventilation (most effective), general ventilation, wet methods (wet drilling, wet grinding — dust suppression with water sprays), mechanisation and automation, dust sampling and environmental monitoring (permissible exposure limits — silica 0.1 mg/m³ respirable, lead 0.05–0.15 mg/m³), personal protective equipment (respirators with appropriate filters, protective clothing, gloves), personal hygiene (washing facilities, change of clothes, no eating, drinking or smoking in workplace — lead), reducing working hours of exposure, rotation of workers; (3) legislative measures — Factories Act 1948 (notifiable diseases include lead poisoning, silicosis, asbestosis, byssinosis, coal miners' pneumoconiosis; welfare, safety provisions), Mines Act 1952, Employees' State Insurance Act 1948 and Employees' Compensation Act 1923 (compensation for occupational diseases listed in schedule), Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code 2020 (consolidates 13 laws), Bureau of Indian Standards, Lead regulation in paints (Household and Decorative Paints Rules 2016 — limit 90 ppm), National Policy on Safety, Health and Environment at Workplace (2009), National Institute of Occupational Health (ICMR-NIOH Ahmedabad), National Human Rights Commission advisories on silicosis.",
[["Pneumoconioses",[["Disease","Dust","Occupations","Key features"],["Silicosis","Free silica","Mining, quarrying, stone cutting, sandblasting, glass, pottery","Upper lobe nodules, snowstorm X-ray, eggshell hilar calcification, ↑ TB"],["Anthracosis (CWP)","Coal","Coal mining","PMF, Caplan's syndrome"],["Asbestosis","Asbestos","Asbestos industry, brake linings, shipbreaking","Lower lobe fibrosis, pleural plaques, mesothelioma, lung cancer"],["Byssinosis","Cotton dust","Textile mills","Monday chest tightness"],["Bagassosis","Bagasse","Sugarcane industry","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis"],["Farmer's lung","Mouldy hay","Agriculture","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis"],["Siderosis","Iron oxide","Welders","Benign"]]],
["Lead poisoning",[["Aspect","Details"],["Sources","Battery manufacture/recycling, smelting, paints, printing, pottery glaze"],["Route","Inhalation (workers), ingestion (children)"],["Features","Colic, constipation, Burtonian line, anaemia with basophilic stippling, wrist/foot drop, encephalopathy, nephropathy"],["Tests","Blood lead, ZPP, urinary ALA, coproporphyrin"],["Treatment","Remove exposure; CaNa2EDTA, DMSA, BAL"]]],
["Prevention",[["Measure","Examples"],["Medical","Pre-placement and periodic exams, X-ray (ILO), spirometry, blood lead"],["Engineering","Substitution, wet methods, enclosure, local exhaust ventilation, monitoring"],["Personal","Respirators, hygiene, no eating at work"],["Legislative","Factories Act, Mines Act, ESI/EC Act, OSH Code 2020"]]]],
{i:"Occupational lung diseases and lead poisoning are entirely preventable but remain common in India's informal mining, stone, textile and battery-recycling sectors.",f:"Respirable dust (0.5–3 µm) → alveolar macrophages → cytokines → fibrosis (silicosis, asbestosis) → ↑ TB, cancer, cor pulmonale\n Lead fumes/dust → blood → bone; inhibits ALA dehydratase, ferrochelatase → anaemia, neuropathy, encephalopathy\n Prevention: engineering > PPE; medical surveillance; legislation",c:"Pneumoconioses — especially silicosis with its tuberculosis risk — and lead poisoning are major occupational diseases; prevention depends on engineering dust and fume control, substitution, personal protection, pre-placement and periodic medical examinations, and enforcement of occupational health legislation."});

Z("cm6",5,"cm",["The Employees' State Insurance (ESI) Act 1948 is a social security scheme providing medical care and cash benefits to employees (earning up to ₹21,000/month) and their dependants in case of sickness, maternity, disablement and death due to employment injury, funded by contributions from employers (3.25%) and employees (0.75%).","ESI is a 'health insurance plus income protection' scheme for organised-sector workers — free treatment and cash when they cannot work.",["Coverage: non-seasonal factories with ≥ 10 persons, shops, hotels, cinemas, educational and medical institutions (≥ 10 employees); employees earning ≤ ₹21,000/month (₹25,000 for persons with disability).","Contributions: employer 3.25% and employee 0.75% of wages (employees earning ≤ ₹176/day exempt from their share); administered by ESI Corporation (Union Labour Minister chairman).","Benefits: medical benefit (full medical care for insured and family — ESI dispensaries/hospitals), sickness benefit (70% wages for up to 91 days/year), extended sickness (80%, up to 2 years — 34 long-term diseases), enhanced sickness (100% for sterilisation), maternity benefit (100% wages for 26 weeks), disablement benefit (temporary 90%; permanent — based on loss of earning capacity), dependants' benefit (90% shared on death from employment injury), funeral expenses (₹15,000), others — unemployment allowance (Rajiv Gandhi Shramik Kalyan Yojana), Atal Bimit Vyakti Kalyan Yojana, vocational rehabilitation, physical rehabilitation.","Merged into Code on Social Security 2020; ESIC hospitals and medical colleges; Abhishek — e-Pehchan card; ESI integration with PM-JAY."],"ESI contributions: employer 3.25% + employee 0.75%; benefits — medical, sickness, maternity, disablement, dependants', funeral."],
"The Employees' State Insurance Act, 1948 was the first major legislation on social security for workers in independent India; it provides for certain benefits to employees in case of sickness, maternity and employment injury and to make provision for related matters. The scheme was first introduced in Kanpur and Delhi on 24 February 1952 (ESI Day). Administration: Employees' State Insurance Corporation (ESIC) — a corporate body with members representing central and state governments, employers, employees, medical profession and Parliament; Union Minister of Labour and Employment is the chairman; Director General is the chief executive; Standing Committee and Medical Benefit Council; medical benefit is administered by state governments (except Delhi and Noida, where ESIC runs directly) through ESI dispensaries, hospitals, panel clinics (insurance medical practitioners) and tie-up hospitals; ESIC also runs medical colleges, PG institutes and dental colleges. Coverage (applicability): non-seasonal factories using power employing 10 or more persons and those not using power employing 20 or more; extended to shops, hotels, restaurants, cinemas including preview theatres, road–motor transport undertakings, newspaper establishments, private medical and educational institutions, employing 10 or more persons (states may lower thresholds); area-wise implementation — now in all districts (> 600 districts); employees drawing wages up to ₹21,000 per month (since 1 January 2017; ₹25,000 for persons with disabilities) are covered; ~3.5 crore insured persons and ~13 crore beneficiaries; under the Code on Social Security 2020 (which subsumes the ESI Act), coverage can be extended to establishments with fewer employees, hazardous occupations (even 1 employee), plantation workers, gig and platform workers (voluntary). Finance: ESI fund formed by contributions — employer's contribution 3.25% and employee's contribution 0.75% of wages (revised from 4.75% and 1.75% in July 2019); employees whose average daily wage is up to ₹176 are exempted from paying their share (employer pays); state governments share one-eighth of medical expenditure (within a ceiling); contribution periods and benefit periods (two of six months each). Benefits: (1) medical benefit — reasonable medical care from day one of insurable employment for insured persons and their family members (spouse, dependent children, parents) — outpatient treatment, domiciliary visits, specialist consultation, hospitalisation, drugs, investigations, dentures, spectacles, artificial limbs and aids; continues for retired and permanently disabled insured persons and spouses on a nominal annual contribution (₹120); no ceiling on expenditure; super-speciality treatment through tie-ups; (2) sickness benefit — periodical cash payments at the rate of 70% of average daily wages for a maximum of 91 days in two consecutive benefit periods (subject to contribution for 78 days in the corresponding contribution period); waiting period of 2 days; certified by insurance medical officer; (3) extended sickness benefit — for 34 long-term diseases (TB, leprosy, cancer, HIV/AIDS, chronic renal failure, mental illnesses, cardiovascular diseases, paralysis etc.) at 80% of wages for up to 2 years (extendable to 3 years); (4) enhanced sickness benefit — 100% of wages for 7 days (vasectomy) or 14 days (tubectomy) for family planning operations; (5) maternity benefit — 100% of average daily wages for 26 weeks (extendable by 1 month on medical advice; 12 weeks for adoptive/commissioning mothers; 6 weeks for miscarriage) — contribution for 70 days in preceding two contribution periods required; confinement expenses ₹7,500 (if delivery occurs where ESI facilities are not available); (6) disablement benefit — (a) temporary disablement benefit at 90% of wages for as long as disability continues (employment injury; from day one of insurable employment, no contribution condition; after 3 days); (b) permanent disablement benefit at 90% of wages paid monthly, proportionate to the loss of earning capacity as certified by Medical Board; (7) dependants' benefit — 90% of wages paid monthly to dependants (widow lifelong/until remarriage; children up to 25 years; daughters until marriage) of an insured person who dies due to employment injury or occupational disease; (8) funeral expenses — ₹15,000 (raised from ₹10,000) to the dependants or person performing last rites; (9) other benefits — unemployment allowance: Rajiv Gandhi Shramik Kalyan Yojana (for insured persons losing jobs due to closure, retrenchment or permanent invalidity — 50% of wages up to 2 years) and Atal Bimit Vyakti Kalyan Yojana (cash relief for unemployed insured persons — 50% of average earnings up to 90 days), vocational rehabilitation, physical rehabilitation, preventive health (immunisation, family planning), skill upgradation, marriage expenses (historical), Ayushman Bharat PM-JAY convergence (ESI beneficiaries can access PM-JAY empanelled hospitals in some areas), ESIC 2.0 reforms (Pehchan card, online services, mobile app). Occupational diseases specified in the Third Schedule (e.g., lead poisoning, silicosis, asbestosis, byssinosis, noise-induced hearing loss, anthrax, occupational cancers) are treated as employment injuries for disablement and dependants' benefits. Role in occupational health: provides comprehensive health care and income security, encourages early reporting of illness and injuries, compensation for occupational diseases, rehabilitation; complements Factories Act (preventive) and Employees' Compensation Act (for non-ESI areas).",
[["Key features",[["Aspect","Details"],["Year","1948 (implemented 1952 — Kanpur, Delhi)"],["Administration","ESI Corporation (Labour Minister chairman)"],["Coverage","Establishments ≥ 10 employees; wages ≤ ₹21,000/month"],["Employer contribution","3.25% of wages"],["Employee contribution","0.75% (exempt if ≤ ₹176/day)"],["Now under","Code on Social Security 2020"]]],
["Benefits",[["Benefit","Rate / duration"],["Medical","Full care for insured + family"],["Sickness","70% wages, 91 days/year"],["Extended sickness","80% wages, up to 2 years (34 diseases)"],["Enhanced sickness","100% for sterilisation (7/14 days)"],["Maternity","100% wages, 26 weeks"],["Temporary disablement","90% wages while disabled"],["Permanent disablement","90% × loss of earning capacity"],["Dependants'","90% wages shared"],["Funeral","₹15,000"],["Unemployment","RGSKY, ABVKY"]]]],
{c:"The ESI Act provides organised-sector workers with comprehensive medical care and cash benefits for sickness, maternity, disablement and death due to employment injury, funded by employer and employee contributions and administered by ESIC, now under the Code on Social Security 2020."});

Z("cm6",6,"cm",["Healthy housing provides shelter that protects against disease and injury and promotes physical, mental and social well-being; criteria include adequate space (floor area, rooms), ventilation, lighting, safe water, sanitation and freedom from overcrowding; ventilation replaces vitiated indoor air with fresh air to maintain comfort and prevent infections.","A healthy house breathes — enough windows and cross-ventilation to change the air, enough light, enough space per person, clean water and a toilet.",["Housing standards (Environmental Hygiene Committee 1949): set back ≥ 1/3 of height, plinth 2–3 ft, room height ≥ 10 ft, floor area of living room ≥ 120 sq ft (multiple rooms ≥ 100 sq ft each), windows ≥ 2/5 of floor area with doors (1/10 at least), lighting — daylight factor ≥ 1% (window area 1/5th floor area), cross-ventilation, safe water, sanitary latrine, kitchen, waste disposal.","Overcrowding indicators: persons per room (1 room — 2 persons, 2 rooms — 3, 3 rooms — 5, 4 rooms — 7, 5+ — 10 + 2 per room), floor space (≥ 110 sq ft — 2 persons…, < 50 sq ft for child ≥ 1 — overcrowded), sex separation (two persons > 10 yr of opposite sex not married to each other sharing a room).","Health effects of poor housing: respiratory infections, TB, meningococcal meningitis, diarrhoea, skin infections (scabies), vector-borne diseases, accidents, mental stress, rodent-borne diseases, indoor air pollution.","Ventilation: natural (wind, stack effect, cross-ventilation) and mechanical (exhaust, plenum/supply, balanced, air conditioning); standards — 3000 cu ft fresh air per person per hour, 2–3 air changes/hour in living rooms; comfort indices — corrected effective temperature, predicted 4-hour sweat rate; sick building syndrome."],"Window area ≥ 1/5 floor area (light), doors + windows ≥ 2/5 (ventilation)."],
"Housing (residential environment) is defined by WHO as including the physical structure that man uses for shelter and the environs of that structure, including all necessary services, facilities, equipment and devices needed or desired for the physical and mental health and social well-being of the family and individual. Importance: housing influences health directly (infections, injuries, chemical exposures) and indirectly (crowding, stress, social factors); housing is a basic need and a social determinant of health; India faces housing shortage (~19 million urban units, 2012) and slums (~6.5 crore people, Census 2011); Pradhan Mantri Awas Yojana (Urban and Gramin, 2015–16) — 'Housing for All'. Criteria for healthful housing (WHO Expert Committee on Public Health Aspects of Housing, 1961): healthful housing provides physical protection and shelter; provides adequately for cooking, eating, washing and excretory functions; is designed, constructed, maintained and used in a manner such as to prevent the spread of communicable diseases; provides for protection from hazards of exposure to noise and pollution; is free from unsafe physical arrangements due to construction or maintenance, and from toxic or harmful materials; and encourages personal and community development, promotes social relationships, reflects a regard for ecological principles and by these means promotes mental health. Housing standards (Environmental Hygiene Committee, India, 1949, and later recommendations): (1) site — elevated from surroundings, well drained, away from nuisance (breeding places, industrial areas, garbage), soil dry and safe, accessibility, pleasant surroundings; (2) set back — open space all round the house ('set back') — not less than one-third the height of the building (to ensure adequate light and ventilation); built area should not exceed one-third of the total plot in rural areas, two-thirds in urban areas; (3) walls — reasonably strong, low heat-carrying capacity, weather resistant, unsuitable for harbouring rats and vermin, smooth without cracks and crevices, 9 inches thick; (4) roof — height not less than 10 ft (3 m) in the case of rooms; heat insulation; (5) floor — impermeable, smooth, free from cracks, easily cleaned, kept dry; plinth (height of floor from ground) 2–3 ft; (6) rooms — number of rooms should not be less than two, one of which at least can be closed for safety and privacy; floor area of living room not less than 120 sq ft (11 m²) where there are more than one room and 100 sq ft (9.3 m²) for each additional room; (7) cubic space — height of room not less than 10 ft, cubic space per person ≥ 500 cu ft (~14 m³) preferred; (8) windows — every living room should have at least two windows, at least one opening directly to an open space; windows placed at a height of not more than 3 ft (1 m) above floor level; window area should be 1/5th of the floor area; doors and windows combined should be 2/5th of floor area; (9) lighting — daylight factor (ratio of indoor illumination at a point to simultaneous outdoor illumination under overcast sky) should exceed 1% in all rooms (living rooms 2%); artificial lighting adequate; (10) kitchen — separate kitchen, adequate light and ventilation (smoke outlet/chimney), fly-proof storage of food, impervious platform; (11) privy (latrine) — sanitary latrine within the premises; (12) garbage and refuse disposal — removed and disposed of by sanitary methods; (13) bathing and washing facilities — provision for privacy; (14) water supply — safe and continuous supply of drinking water. Indicators of housing: (a) structure — type of house, kutcha/pucca; (b) overcrowding — (i) persons per room: 1 room — 2 persons; 2 rooms — 3; 3 rooms — 5; 4 rooms — 7; 5 or more rooms — 10 (with additional 2 for each further room); in calculation, children under 1 year not counted, 1–10 years counted as half; (ii) floor space: ≥ 110 sq ft — 2 persons; 90–100 sq ft — 1.5 persons; 70–90 sq ft — 1 person; 50–70 sq ft — 0.5 person; < 50 sq ft — nil (no occupancy); (iii) sex separation — overcrowding exists when two persons over 10 years of age of opposite sex not husband and wife have to sleep in the same room; overcrowding is defined in terms of persons per room, floor space per person and sex separation; (c) amenities — water supply, sanitation, electricity, cooking fuel; (d) occupancy ratio; (e) proportion of slum population. Health effects of poor housing: respiratory infections (common cold, pneumonia, TB — overcrowding and poor ventilation promote droplet and airborne spread; also diphtheria, whooping cough, measles, meningococcal meningitis, influenza, COVID-19), skin and eye infections (scabies, ringworm, impetigo, trachoma — inadequate water and overcrowding), diarrhoeal diseases and worm infestations (poor sanitation, unsafe water), rat infestation (plague, leptospirosis, rat bite fever), vector-borne diseases (malaria, dengue — water storage, poor drainage), arthropod infestation (bedbugs, lice), accidents and injuries (falls, burns, electrical), indoor air pollution (biomass fuels — ARI, COPD, lung cancer, cataract), dampness and mould (asthma, allergies), lead poisoning (old paint), psychosocial effects (stress, lack of privacy, domestic violence, mental illness, juvenile delinquency, social isolation), high morbidity and mortality, especially among children and elderly; homelessness associated with very poor health. Ventilation: means not merely the replacement of vitiated air by a supply of fresh outdoor air, but also control of the quality of incoming air with regard to its temperature, humidity and purity, with a view to provide a thermal environment that is comfortable and free from risk of infection; indoor air is vitiated by respiration and activities — rise in temperature and humidity, accumulation of CO2, body odours, dust, bacteria, smoke; standards of ventilation: (a) cubic space — 1000–1200 cu ft per person (sufficient for 3000 cu ft fresh air/hr with 2–3 air changes); recommended minimum air space per person 500 cu ft (Indian), (b) air change — in living rooms 2–3 air changes per hour; in workrooms and assembly halls 4–6 air changes; fresh air supply of 3000 cu ft per person per hour (~85 m³/h); (c) floor space — 50–100 sq ft per person; (d) cross-ventilation (windows on opposite walls) and window area ≥ 1/5th of floor area. Types of ventilation: (1) natural ventilation — (i) wind (perflation — wind blowing through doors and windows; aspiration — suction effect), (ii) diffusion of gases through openings, (iii) inequality of temperature (stack/convection effect — warm air rises and escapes through upper openings, cool fresh air enters below — e.g., ventilators near ceiling, cowls); cross-ventilation is the most important factor in natural ventilation; (2) mechanical (artificial) ventilation — (i) exhaust ventilation (air is extracted by exhaust fans — used in kitchens, toilets, factories (local exhaust for dust and fumes), assembly halls; fresh air enters through inlets), (ii) plenum ventilation (supply ventilation — fresh air is blown into the room by centrifugal fans, displacing vitiated air through outlets), (iii) balanced ventilation (combination of supply and exhaust), (iv) air conditioning (simultaneous control of temperature, humidity, air movement and purity — temperature 20–25 °C, relative humidity 30–60%, air movement 20–50 ft/min). Thermal comfort indices: air temperature, humidity, air movement, radiant heat combined in indices — effective temperature, corrected effective temperature (CET — uses globe thermometer; comfort zone 20–25 °C in India), predicted four-hour sweat rate (P4SR — best index of heat stress), heat index, wet bulb globe temperature (WBGT — occupational heat stress); comfort zone in India 25–27 °C CET. Sick building syndrome (in sealed, air-conditioned buildings with poor fresh air exchange) — headache, fatigue, eye and throat irritation, attributed to VOCs, moulds, inadequate ventilation; building-related illnesses — Legionnaires' disease (cooling towers).",
[["Housing standards (India)",[["Element","Standard"],["Set back","≥ 1/3 of building height"],["Plinth","2–3 ft"],["Room height","≥ 10 ft"],["Floor area","≥ 120 sq ft (living room); ≥ 100 sq ft each additional room"],["Windows","2 per room; ≤ 3 ft from floor; area 1/5 of floor"],["Doors + windows","2/5 of floor area"],["Daylight factor","> 1%"],["Amenities","Kitchen, sanitary latrine, safe water, waste disposal"]]],
["Overcrowding — persons per room",[["Rooms","Persons"],["1","2"],["2","3"],["3","5"],["4","7"],["5+","10 + 2 per additional room"],["Counting","< 1 yr not counted; 1–10 yr = ½"]]],
["Ventilation types",[["Type","Mechanism"],["Natural","Wind (perflation, aspiration), diffusion, stack effect, cross-ventilation"],["Exhaust","Fans extract vitiated air (kitchens, factories)"],["Plenum","Fans blow fresh air in"],["Balanced","Supply + exhaust"],["Air conditioning","Controls temperature, humidity, movement, purity"]]],
["Health effects of poor housing",["Respiratory infections, TB","Skin/eye infections (scabies, trachoma)","Diarrhoea, worms","Rodent- and vector-borne diseases","Accidents, burns","Indoor air pollution diseases","Mental stress, lack of privacy"]]],
{c:"Healthy housing with adequate space, lighting, cross-ventilation, water and sanitation prevents overcrowding-related infections and promotes well-being; housing standards and overcrowding indicators guide assessment, while natural and mechanical ventilation maintain healthy indoor air."});
